{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22954","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22954","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22954","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liwei","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Liwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanjun","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Yuanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Margaret","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Margaret","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mickel","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mickel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond","familyName":"Fok","name":"Fok, Raymond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nouha","familyName":"Dziri","name":"Dziri, Nouha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulia","familyName":"Tsvetkov","name":"Tsvetkov, Yulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"Sap","name":"Sap, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alon","familyName":"Albalak","name":"Albalak, Alon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial Hivemind: The Open-Ended Homogeneity of Language Models (and Beyond)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:16:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:18:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-06T17:33:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-07T01:27:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models (LMs) often struggle to generate diverse, human-like creative content, raising concerns about the long-term homogenization of human thought through repeated exposure to similar outputs. Yet scalable methods for evaluating LM output diversity remain limited, especially beyond narrow tasks such as random number or name generation, or beyond repeated sampling from a single model. We introduce Infinity-Chat, a large-scale dataset of 26K diverse, real-world, open-ended user queries that admit a wide range of plausible answers with no single ground truth. We introduce the first comprehensive taxonomy for characterizing the full spectrum of open-ended prompts posed to LMs, comprising 6 top-level categories (e.g., brainstorm \u0026amp; ideation) that further breaks down to 17 subcategories. Using Infinity-Chat, we present a large-scale study of mode collapse in LMs, revealing a pronounced Artificial Hivemind effect in open-ended generation of LMs, characterized by (1) intra-model repetition, where a single model consistently generates similar responses, and more so (2) inter-model homogeneity, where different models produce strikingly similar outputs. Infinity-Chat also includes 31,250 human annotations, across absolute ratings and pairwise preferences, with 25 independent human annotations per example. This enables studying collective and individual-specific human preferences in response to open-ended queries. Our findings show that LMs, reward models, and LM judges are less well calibrated to human ratings on model generations that elicit differing idiosyncratic annotator preferences, despite maintaining comparable overall quality. Overall, INFINITY-CHAT presents the first large-scale resource for systematically studying real-world open-ended queries to LMs, revealing critical insights to guide future research for mitigating long-term AI safety risks posed by the Artificial Hivemind."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 D\u0026amp;B Paper (Oral); Camera-Ready Version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22954","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:31Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:16:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22009","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22009","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22009","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqin","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yangqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenPhone: Mobile Agentic Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T20:23:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:12:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-07T17:26:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T01:42:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T01:17:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:47:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the advancement of multimodal large language models (MLLMs), building GUI agent systems has become an increasingly promising direction--especially for mobile platforms, given their rich app ecosystems and intuitive touch interactions. Yet mobile GUI agents face a critical dilemma: truly on-device models (4B or smaller) lack sufficient performance, while capable models (starting from 7B) are either too large for mobile deployment or prohibitively costly (e.g., cloud-only closed-source MLLMs). To resolve this, we propose OpenPhone, a mobile GUI agent system that leverages device-cloud collaboration to tap the cost-efficiency of on device models and the high capability of cloud models, while avoiding their drawbacks. Specifically, OpenPhone enhances Qwen2.5-VL-3B via two-stage SFT-\u0026gt;GRPO training on synthetic GUI data for strong decision-making, integrates an efficient long-reasoning and memory management mechanism to utilize historical interactions under tight resources, and defaults to on-device execution--only escalating challenging subtasks to the cloud via real-time complexity assessment. Experiments on the online AndroidLab benchmark and diverse apps show OpenPhone matches or nears larger models, with a significant reduction in cloud costs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22009","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:05:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:05:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:42:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Issa","familyName":"Sugiura","name":"Sugiura, Issa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhei","familyName":"Kurita","name":"Kurita, Shuhei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Oda","name":"Oda, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisuke","familyName":"Kawahara","name":"Kawahara, Daisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasuo","familyName":"Okabe","name":"Okabe, Yasuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoaki","familyName":"Okazaki","name":"Okazaki, Naoaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WAON: A Large-Scale Japanese Image-Text Dataset for Cultural Adaptation in Contrastive Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T12:42:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:35:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-01T12:35:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:49:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-31T10:16:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:07:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-02T02:27:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:25:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contrastive vision-language models have achieved remarkable progress through large-scale pretraining. Recent work has shown that removing English-only caption filters and pretraining on global data is effective for improving multicultural performance. We study whether such global pretraining is sufficient for culture-specific understanding, or whether further adaptation with natively sourced data can boost performance beyond what global pretraining alone achieves. To enable this investigation, we present WAON, the largest publicly available native Japanese image-text dataset constructed from native Japanese web content in Common Crawl, containing approximately 155 million examples. We also introduce WAON-Bench, a manually curated Japanese cultural benchmark spanning 374 classes. Through comparative fine-tuning experiments on multiple Japanese image-text datasets, we observe that models fine-tuned on WAON consistently achieve stronger performance on Japanese cultural benchmarks than those fine-tuned on English-to-Japanese translated data. Controlled experiments at matched scale, filtering, and training budget across two model families further indicate that native web origin is the primary driver of this gain. We release our dataset, benchmark, model, and code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AACL 2026 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:11:31Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:11:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:43:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23576","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23576","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23576","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiazhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UrbanVLA: A Vision-Language-Action Model for Urban Micromobility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:46:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:58:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T02:14:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T00:35:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Urban micromobility applications, such as delivery robots, demand reliable navigation across large-scale urban environments while following long-horizon route instructions. This task is particularly challenging due to the dynamic and unstructured nature of real-world city areas, yet most existing navigation methods remain tailored to short-scale and controllable scenarios. Effective urban micromobility requires two complementary levels of navigation skills: low-level capabilities such as point-goal reaching and obstacle avoidance, and high-level capabilities, such as route-visual alignment. To this end, we propose UrbanVLA, a route-conditioned Vision-Language-Action (VLA) framework designed for scalable urban navigation. Our method explicitly aligns noisy route waypoints with visual observations during execution, and subsequently plans trajectories to drive the robot. To enable UrbanVLA to master both levels of navigation, we employ a two-stage training pipeline. The process begins with Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) using simulated environments and trajectories parsed from web videos. This is followed by Reinforcement Fine-Tuning (RFT) on a mixture of simulation and real-world data, which enhances the model's safety and adaptability in real-world settings. Experiments demonstrate that UrbanVLA surpasses strong baselines by more than 55% in the SocialNav task on MetaUrban. Furthermore, UrbanVLA achieves reliable real-world navigation, showcasing both scalability to large-scale urban environments and robustness against real-world uncertainties."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23576","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:25Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:28:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22752","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22752","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22752","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anooshka","familyName":"Bajaj","name":"Bajaj, Anooshka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deven Mahesh","familyName":"Mistry","name":"Mistry, Deven Mahesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahaj Singh","familyName":"Maini","name":"Maini, Sahaj Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yash","familyName":"Aggarwal","name":"Aggarwal, Yash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zoran","familyName":"Tiganj","name":"Tiganj, Zoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Semantics: How Temporal Biases Shape Retrieval in Transformer and State-Space Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:01:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:06:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-29T14:27:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-30T01:35:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In-context learning is governed by both temporal and semantic relationships, shaping how Large Language Models (LLMs) retrieve contextual information. Analogous to human episodic memory, where the retrieval of specific events is enabled by separating events that happened at different times, this work probes the ability of various pretrained LLMs, including transformer and state-space models, to differentiate and retrieve temporally separated events. Specifically, we prompted models with sequences containing multiple presentations of the same token, which reappears at the sequence end. By fixing the positions of these repeated tokens and permuting all others, we removed semantic confounds and isolated temporal effects on next-token prediction. Across diverse sequences, models consistently placed the highest probabilities on tokens following a repeated token, but with a notable bias for those nearest the beginning or end of the input. An ablation experiment linked this phenomenon in transformers to induction heads. Extending the analysis to unique semantic contexts with partial overlap further demonstrated that memories embedded in the middle of a prompt are retrieved less reliably. Despite architectural differences, state-space and transformer models showed comparable temporal biases. Our findings deepen the understanding of temporal biases in in-context learning and offer an illustration of how these biases can enable temporal separation and episodic retrieval."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22752","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:22:45Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:22:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-30T04:01:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hetian","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Hetian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CityRiSE: Reasoning Urban Socio-Economic Status in Large Vision-Language Models via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T12:56:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:35:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T11:41:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:44:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Urban socio-economic sensing plays a vital role in advancing global sustainable development goals. With the advent of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), new opportunities have emerged to address this challenge by framing it as a multi-modal perception and reasoning task. However, recent studies show that LVLMs still struggle to make accurate and interpretable socio-economic predictions from visual data. To overcome these limitations and fully exploit the potential of LVLMs, we propose CityRiSE, a novel framework for Reasoning urban Socio-Economic status in LVLMs via reinforcement learning (RL). With carefully curated multi-modal dataset and verifiable reward design, our approach guides the LVLM to focus on semantically meaningful visual cues, enabling structured and goal-oriented reasoning for generalist socio-economic status prediction. Experiments demonstrate that CityRiSE, equipped with emergent reasoning, significantly outperforms existing baselines, improving both prediction accuracy and generalization across diverse urban contexts, especially on unseen cities and unseen indicators. This work highlights the promise of combining RL and LVLMs for interpretable and generalist urban socio-economic sensing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM MM 2026, https://github.com/tsinghua-fib-lab/CityRiSE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:11:39Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:11:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:23:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22665","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22665","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22665","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiwei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Qiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xukun","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puhong","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Puhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xudong","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Xudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shutao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SARVLM: A Vision Language Foundation Model for Semantic Understanding in SAR Imagery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T13:04:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:00:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-26T10:05:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-27T01:38:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T05:24:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-18T00:27:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-08T09:11:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:27:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) is a critical imaging modality due to its all-weather operational capability. Although recent advances in self-supervised learning and masked image modeling (MIM) have enabled SAR foundation models, these approaches primarily focus on low-level visual features and often neglect multi-modal representation. Moreover, multimodal data for SAR is scarce, limiting the development of robust cross-modal models. To address this limitation, we construct SARVLM-1M, a large-scale vision-language dataset comprising over one million image-text pairs aggregated from existing datasets. Furthermore, to mitigate the substantial differences between SAR and natural imagery, we propose a two-stage domain transfer training strategy that leverages optical remote sensing data as an intermediate bridge, facilitating effective knowledge transfer from natural images to SAR domains. Based on this strategy, we develop SARVLM, the first vision-language foundation model tailored for SAR, consisting of SARCLIP and SARCap. In addition, an ensemble strategy is utilized to improve the cross-scene generalization capability of the model. Moreover, SARDet and SARRot further validate the capability of the proposed framework in object detection. Extensive experiments on 13 benchmarks across image-text retrieval, target recognition, zero-shot classification, object detection, semantic localization, and image captioning demonstrate the superior feature extraction and interpretation capabilities of SARVLM. It consistently outperforms state-of-the-art vision-language models and advances semantic understanding in SAR imagery. Code and datasets will be released on https://github.com/KlayMa527/SARVLM.git."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22665","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:20:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:20:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22014","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22014","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22014","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Ball","name":"Ball, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niki","familyName":"Hasrati","name":"Hasrati, Niki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Robey","name":"Robey, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avi","familyName":"Schwarzschild","name":"Schwarzschild, Avi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frauke","familyName":"Kreuter","name":"Kreuter, Frauke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zico","familyName":"Kolter","name":"Kolter, Zico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrej","familyName":"Risteski","name":"Risteski, Andrej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Understanding the Transferability of Adversarial Suffixes in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T20:28:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:12:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-04T16:53:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-05T01:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discrete optimization-based jailbreaking attacks on large language models aim to generate short, nonsensical suffixes that, when appended onto input prompts, elicit disallowed content. Notably, these suffixes are often transferable -- succeeding on prompts and models for which they were never optimized. And yet, despite the fact that transferability is surprising and empirically well-established, the field lacks a rigorous analysis of when and why transfer occurs. To fill this gap, we identify three statistical properties that strongly correlate with transfer success across numerous experimental settings: (1) how much a prompt without a suffix activates a model's internal refusal direction, (2) how strongly a suffix induces a push away from this direction, and (3) how large these shifts are in directions orthogonal to refusal. On the other hand, we find that prompt semantic similarity only weakly correlates with transfer success. These findings lead to a more fine-grained understanding of transferability, which we use in interventional experiments to showcase how our statistical analysis can translate into practical improvements in attack success."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at TMLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22014","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:05:16Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:05:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-05T02:30:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22868","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22868","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22868","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Imani","name":"Imani, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing the Unseen: Towards Training-Free Inspection for Wind Turbine Blades Using Knowledge-Augmented Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T23:19:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:12:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-01T15:11:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:37:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Wind turbine blades operate in harsh environments, making timely damage detection essential for preventing failures and optimizing maintenance. Drone-based inspection and deep learning are promising, but typically depend on large, labeled datasets, which limit their ability to detect rare or evolving damage types. To address this, we propose a zero-shot-oriented inspection framework that integrates Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with Vision-Language Models (VLM). A multimodal knowledge base is constructed, comprising technical documentation, representative reference images, and domain-specific guidelines. A hybrid text-image retriever with keyword-aware reranking assembles the most relevant context to condition the VLM at inference, injecting domain knowledge without task-specific training. We evaluate the framework on 30 labeled blade images covering diverse damage categories. Although the dataset is small due to the difficulty of acquiring verified blade imagery, it covers multiple representative defect types. On this test set, the RAG-grounded VLM correctly classified all samples, whereas the same VLM without retrieval performed worse in both accuracy and precision. We further compare against open-vocabulary baselines and incorporate uncertainty Clopper-Pearson confidence intervals to account for the small-sample setting. Ablation studies indicate that the key advantage of the framework lies in explainability and generalizability: retrieved references ground the reasoning process and enable the detection of previously unseen defects by leveraging domain knowledge rather than relying solely on visual cues. This research contributes a data-efficient solution for industrial inspection that reduces dependence on extensive labeled datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22868","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:36:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21770","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21770","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21770","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Baek","name":"Baek, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Numerical Fragility in Transformers: A Layer-wise Theory for Risk Estimation and Selective Stabilization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-17T01:03:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:01:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T11:29:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T00:34:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-precision execution can induce substantial forward discrepancies in Transformers even for fixed weights and input, yet these discrepancies are usually monitored only at the output and lack a layer-wise theoretical account. We develop a first-order decomposition of output mismatch into layer-local attention, LayerNorm, and residual-transport terms, and derive from it a practical causal risk estimator and a budgeted controller, Bound-Guided Selective Stabilization (BGSS). Controlled sweeps verify the predicted local sign, monotonicity, and transport structure. On GPT-2, the transport-aware combined predictor is positively correlated with FP32-reference mismatch in all $18$ runs and improves over a no-transport ablation in $17/18$ runs. Reference-patch attribution shows that the same score preserves useful layer ordering information (mean Spearman $0.362$). In budget-matched mitigation, BGSS outperforms random same-budget control in onset events ($10.67$ vs. $11.67$), final mismatch ($1.243\\times 10^{-3}$ vs. $1.284\\times 10^{-3}$), and worst-case mismatch ($3.14\\times 10^{-3}$ vs. $8.49\\times 10^{-3}$), while matching a risk-only same-budget controller on onset suppression and sharply reducing worst-case mismatch ($3.14\\times 10^{-3}$ vs. $5.71\\times 10^{-3}$). These results support a theory-to-algorithm account of Transformer numerical fragility in which finite-precision risk can be analyzed, estimated, localized, and selectively stabilized."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 10 figures. Accepted at the 29th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS 2026). Camera-ready version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21770","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T02:59:31Z","registered":"2025-10-28T02:59:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-27T01:52:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22338","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22338","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22338","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aritra","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Aritra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srijoni","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, Srijoni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anamitra","familyName":"Mukhopadhyay","name":"Mukhopadhyay, Anamitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Partha Pratim","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Partha Pratim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul D","familyName":"Clough","name":"Clough, Paul D","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Partha Pratim","familyName":"Chakrabarti","name":"Chakrabarti, Partha Pratim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Design-Aware Context in Large Language Models for Code Comment Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T15:44:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:39:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T14:59:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:47:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-04T05:38:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-21T01:42:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3786582.3786825","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Comments are very useful to the flow of code development. With the increasing commonality of code, novice coders have been creating a significant amount of codebases. Due to lack of commenting standards, their comments are often useless, and increase the time taken to further maintain codes. This study intends to find the usefulness of large language models (LLMs) in these cases to generate potentially better comments. This study focuses on the feasibility of design documents as a context for the LLMs to generate more useful comments, as design documents are often used by maintainers to understand code when comments do not suffice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22338","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:00Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:43:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23497","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23497","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23497","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walid","familyName":"Bousselham","name":"Bousselham, Walid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hilde","familyName":"Kuehne","name":"Kuehne, Hilde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cordelia","familyName":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Cordelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VOLD: Reasoning Transfer from LLMs to Vision-Language Models via On-Policy Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:32:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T11:09:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:45:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-03T18:11:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:05:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training vision-language models (VLMs) for complex reasoning remains a challenging task, i.a. due to the scarcity of high-quality image-text reasoning data. Conversely, text-based reasoning resources are abundant and scalable, but it is still an open question how to leveraging them for VLM reasoning. To address this problem, we propose VOLD, a framework to transfer reasoning capabilities from text-only teacher models to VLM student models. To this end, VOLD combines reinforcement learning via Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with on-policy distillation, which allows the student reasoning traces to be guided by the teacher model, resulting in a significant gain over using GRPO alone. We further show that a cold-start alignment is essential for an effective transfer during the online training phase in this scenario and that without sufficient distributional alignment between teacher and student, on-policy distillation fails to provide meaningful guidance. We evaluate VOLD across diverse benchmarks including MMMU-Pro, MathVision, MathVista, and LogicVista, showing that VOLD outperforms the baseline model significantly and improves over the state of the art by a margin. Our ablation shows the importance of a cold-start alignment via SFT for on-policy distillation with a text-only teacher."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"www.walidbousselham.com/VOLD/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23497","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22768","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22768","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22768","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav Narayanan","familyName":"Venkit","name":"Venkit, Pranav Narayanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kung-Hsiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kung-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Sheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chien-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing is Believing? Evaluating Vision-Language Model Susceptibility in Agent-to-Agent Multimodal Persuasion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:39:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-02T23:23:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:01:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As autonomous agents increasingly interact, they inevitably attempt to influence one another. While prior work in text-only settings has explored the dynamics of Agent-to-Agent (A2A) persuasion, the rise of Vision-Language Models (VLMs) introduces a more complex challenge: multimodal content conveys richer information while integrating subtle, hard-to-detect persuasive cues. To study this vulnerability, we present MMPersuade, a unified framework and dataset for A2A multimodal persuasion. We model interactions between a persuader agent, which leverages images and psychological strategies, and a persuadee VLM. Our benchmark spans commercial, subjective and behavioral, and adversarial contexts, and evaluates persuasion via function-calling that capture behavioral shifts beyond verbal responses. Experiments on six VLMs reveal three findings: (1) multimodal inputs consistently outperform text-only persuasion, with raw visual signals uniquely increasing susceptibility in adversarial settings by bypassing text-activated safety defenses; (2) persuadee vulnerability is highly domain- and format-dependent, with realistic and community-style formats driving susceptibility in commercial settings while different formats dominate in adversarial ones; and (3) psychological strategy efficacy varies with context and model architecture, as more capable models resist benign persuasion yet become more susceptible under adversarial multimodal inputs. Our framework provides a foundation for building more robust and aligned VLMs in multi-agent environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22768","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:08Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suchan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Suchan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihoon","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Jihoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sohyeon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sohyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minseok","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Minseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bong-Gyu","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Bong-Gyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hwanjo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Hwanjo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soyeon Caren","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Soyeon Caren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MAP4TS: A Multi-Aspect Prompting Framework for Time-Series Forecasting with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T07:51:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:28:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-21T14:06:12Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; There is a error in modeling. Thereafter, paper will be revised and re-uploaded"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:59:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances have investigated the use of pretrained large language models (LLMs) for time-series forecasting by aligning numerical inputs with LLM embedding spaces. However, existing multimodal approaches often overlook the distinct statistical properties and temporal dependencies that are fundamental to time-series data. To bridge this gap, we propose MAP4TS, a novel Multi-Aspect Prompting Framework that explicitly incorporates classical time-series analysis into the prompt design. Our framework introduces four specialized prompt components: a Global Domain Prompt that conveys dataset-level context, a Local Domain Prompt that encodes recent trends and series-specific behaviors, and a pair of Statistical and Temporal Prompts that embed handcrafted insights derived from autocorrelation (ACF), partial autocorrelation (PACF), and Fourier analysis. Multi-Aspect Prompts are combined with raw time-series embeddings and passed through a cross-modality alignment module to produce unified representations, which are then processed by an LLM and projected for final forecasting. Extensive experiments across eight diverse datasets show that MAP4TS consistently outperforms state-of-the-art LLM-based methods. Our ablation studies further reveal that prompt-aware designs significantly enhance performance stability and that GPT-2 backbones, when paired with structured prompts, outperform larger models like LLaMA in long-term forecasting tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"There is a error in modeling. Thereafter, paper will be revised and re-uploaded"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-22T02:30:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23254","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23254","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23254","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengyao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tengyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard J.","familyName":"Samworth","name":"Samworth, Richard J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal In-context Adaptivity and Distributional Robustness of Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62G08, 68T07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:16:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:39:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-25T13:42:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:52:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-07T10:47:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-08T00:48:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study in-context learning problems where a Transformer is pretrained on tasks drawn from a mixture distribution $π=\\sum_{α\\in\\mathcal{A}} λ_α π_α$, called the pretraining prior, in which each mixture component $π_α$ is a distribution on tasks of a specific difficulty level indexed by $α$. Our goal is to understand the performance of the pretrained Transformer when evaluated on a different test distribution $μ$, consisting of tasks of fixed difficulty $β\\in\\mathcal{A}$, and with potential distribution shift relative to $π_β$, subject to the chi-squared divergence $χ^2(μ,π_β)$ being at most $κ$. In particular, we consider nonparametric regression problems with random smoothness, and multi-index models with both random smoothness and random effective dimension. We prove that a large Transformer pretrained on sufficient data achieves the optimal rate of convergence corresponding to the difficulty level $β$, uniformly over test distributions $μ$ in the chi-squared divergence ball. Thus, the pretrained Transformer is able to achieve faster rates of convergence on easier tasks and is robust to distribution shift at test time. Finally, we prove that even if an estimator had access to the test distribution $μ$, the convergence rate of its expected risk over $μ$ could not be faster than that of our pretrained Transformers, thereby providing a more appropriate optimality guarantee than minimax lower bounds."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23254","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:34:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:34:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-08T12:52:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuejing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuejing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibo","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Sibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruibing","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Ruibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Junyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting Multimodal Positional Encoding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T08:00:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:28:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-05T14:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:46:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T07:18:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:16:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal position encoding is essential for vision-language models, yet there has been little systematic investigation into multimodal position encoding. We conduct a comprehensive analysis of multimodal Rotary Positional Embedding (RoPE) by examining its two core components: position design and frequency allocation. Through extensive experiments, we identify three key guidelines: positional coherence, full frequency utilization, and preservation of textual priors-ensuring unambiguous layout, rich representation, and faithful transfer from the pre-trained LLM. Based on these insights, we propose Multi-Head RoPE (MHRoPE) and MRoPE-Interleave (MRoPE-I), two simple and plug-and-play variants that require no architectural changes. Our methods consistently outperform existing approaches across diverse benchmarks, with significant improvements in both general and fine-grained multimodal understanding. Code will be avaliable at https://github.com/JJJYmmm/Multimodal-RoPEs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-07T03:27:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeongkyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyeongkyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orestis","familyName":"Oikonomou","name":"Oikonomou, Orestis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ZeroFlood: Flood Hazard Mapping from Single-Modality SAR Using Geo-Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T14:14:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:45:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-30T22:59:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:12:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Flood hazard mapping is essential for disaster prevention but remains challenging in data-scarce regions, where traditional hydrodynamic models require extensive geophysical inputs. This paper introduces \\textit{ZeroFlood}, a framework that leverages Geo-Foundation Models (GeoFMs) to predict flood hazard maps using single-modality Earth Observation (EO) data, specifically SAR imagery. We construct a dataset that pairs EO data with flood hazard simulations across the European continent. Using this dataset, we evaluate several recent GeoFMs for the flood hazard segmentation task. Experimental results show that the best-performing model, TerraMind, achieves an F1-score of 88.36\\%, outperforming supervised learning baselines by more than 3 percentage points. We shows the performance can be further improved by applying the Thinking-in-Modality (TiM) mechanism. These results demonstrate the potential of Geo-Foundation Models for data-driven flood hazard mapping using limited observational inputs. The dataset and experiment code are publicly available at https://github.com/khyeongkyun/zeroflood."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16th European Conference on Synthetic Aperture Radar"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:37:19Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:37:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22201","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22201","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22201","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minho","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Minho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kinam","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kinam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junha","familyName":"Hyung","name":"Hyung, Junha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyojin","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Hyojin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoiyeong","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Hoiyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jooyeol","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Jooyeol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hojoon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hojoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaegul","familyName":"Choo","name":"Choo, Jaegul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ACG: Action Coherence Guidance for Flow-based Vision-Language-Action models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T07:44:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:29:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T14:00:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:56:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion and flow matching models have emerged as powerful robot policies, enabling Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models to generalize across diverse scenes and instructions. Yet, when trained via imitation learning, their high generative capacity makes them sensitive to noise in human demonstrations: jerks, pauses, and jitter which reduce action coherence. Reduced action coherence causes instability and trajectory drift during deployment, failures that are catastrophic in fine-grained manipulation where precision is crucial. In this paper, we present Action Coherence Guidance (ACG) for VLA models, a training-free test-time guidance algorithm that improves action coherence and thereby yields performance gains. Evaluated on RoboCasa, DexMimicGen, and real-world SO-101 tasks, ACG consistently improves action coherence and boosts success rates across diverse manipulation tasks. Code and project page are available at https://github.com/DAVIAN-Robotics/ACG and https://DAVIAN-Robotics.github.io/ACG , respectively."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICRA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22201","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:09:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:09:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:30:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23421","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23421","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23421","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Pirrone","name":"Pirrone, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Fricano","name":"Fricano, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gioacchino","familyName":"Fazio","name":"Fazio, Gioacchino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying Systemic Vulnerability in the Foundation Model Industry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T15:26:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:50:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-24T16:13:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T01:12:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The foundation model industry exhibits unprecedented concentration in critical inputs: semiconductors, energy infrastructure, elite talent, capital, and training data. Despite extensive sectoral analyses, no comprehensive framework exists for assessing overall industrial vulnerability. We develop the Artificial Intelligence Industrial Vulnerability Index (AIIVI) grounded in O-Ring production theory, recognizing that foundation model production requires simultaneous availability of non-substitutable inputs. Given extreme data opacity and rapid technological evolution, we implement a validated human-in-the-loop methodology using large language models to systematically extract indicators from dispersed grey literature, with complete human verification of all outputs. Applied to six state-of-the-art foundation model developers, AIIVI equals 0.82, indicating extreme vulnerability driven by compute infrastructure (0.85) and energy systems (0.90). While industrial policy currently emphasizes semiconductor capacity, energy infrastructure represents the emerging binding constraint. This methodology proves applicable to other fast-evolving, opaque industries where traditional data sources are inadequate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Conference Paper - SIEPI (29-30 January 2026) - Bari"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23421","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:38:40Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:38:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-25T02:32:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22272","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22272","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22272","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tu Anh","familyName":"Dinh","name":"Dinh, Tu Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp Nicolas","familyName":"Schumacher","name":"Schumacher, Philipp Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Niehues","name":"Niehues, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Slides to Chatbots: Enhancing Large Language Models with University Course Materials"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T12:31:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:35:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T09:03:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:58:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have advanced rapidly in recent years. One application of LLMs is to support student learning in educational settings. However, prior work has shown that LLMs still struggle to answer questions accurately within university-level computer science courses. In this work, we investigate how incorporating university course materials can enhance LLM performance in this setting. A key challenge lies in leveraging diverse course materials such as lecture slides and transcripts, which differ substantially from typical textual corpora: slides also contain visual elements like images and formulas, while transcripts contain spoken, less structured language. We compare two strategies, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and Continual Pre-Training (CPT), to extend LLMs with course-specific knowledge. For lecture slides, we further explore a multi-modal RAG approach, where we present the retrieved content to the generator in image form. Our experiments reveal that, given the relatively small size of university course materials, RAG is more effective and efficient than CPT. Moreover, incorporating slides as images in the multi-modal setting significantly improves performance over text-only retrieval. These findings highlight practical strategies for developing AI assistants that better support learning and teaching, and we hope they inspire similar efforts in other educational contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NSLP @ LREC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22272","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:11:25Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:11:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:55:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23410","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23410","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23410","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeshu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yeshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushen","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Yushen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhilin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bid2X: Revealing Dynamics of Bidding Environment in Online Advertising from A Foundation Model Lens"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T15:15:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:49:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-14T11:52:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T00:46:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Auto-bidding is crucial in facilitating online advertising by automatically providing bids for advertisers. While previous work has made great efforts to model bidding environments for better ad performance, it has limitations in generalizability across environments since these models are typically tailored for specific bidding scenarios. To this end, we approach the scenario-independent principles through a unified function that estimates the achieved effect under specific bids, such as budget consumption, gross merchandise volume (GMV), page views, etc. Then, we propose a bidding foundation model Bid2X to learn this fundamental function from data in various scenarios. Our Bid2X is built over uniform series embeddings that encode heterogeneous data through tailored embedding methods. To capture complex inter-variable and dynamic temporal dependencies in bidding data, we propose two attention mechanisms separately treating embeddings of different variables and embeddings at different times as attention tokens for representation learning. On top of the learned variable and temporal representations, a variable-aware fusion module is used to perform adaptive bidding outcome prediction. To model the unique bidding data distribution, we devise a zero-inflated projection module to incorporate the estimated non-zero probability into its value prediction, which makes up a joint optimization objective containing classification and regression. The objective is proven to converge to the zero-inflated distribution. Our model has been deployed on the ad platform in Taobao, one of the world's largest e-commerce platforms. Offline evaluation on eight datasets exhibits Bid2X's superiority compared to various baselines and its generality across different scenarios. Bid2X increased GMV by 4.65% and ROI by 2.44% in online A/B tests, paving the way for bidding foundation model in computational advertising."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, KDD 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23410","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:38:25Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:38:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T04:57:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22758","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22758","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22758","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lutong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Lutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zefeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Ao","name":"Ao, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EchoMind: An Interrelated Multi-level Benchmark for Evaluating Empathetic Speech Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:15:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:06:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-05T07:37:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T01:37:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech Language Models (SLMs) have made significant progress in spoken language understanding. Yet it remains unclear whether they can fully perceive non lexical vocal cues alongside spoken words, and respond with empathy that aligns with both emotional and contextual factors. Existing benchmarks typically evaluate linguistic, acoustic, reasoning, or dialogue abilities in isolation, overlooking the integration of these skills that is crucial for human-like, emotionally intelligent conversation. We present EchoMind, the first interrelated, multi-level benchmark that simulates the cognitive process of empathetic dialogue through sequential, context-linked tasks: spoken-content understanding, vocal-cue perception, integrated reasoning, and response generation. All tasks share identical and semantically neutral scripts that are free of explicit emotional or contextual cues, and controlled variations in vocal style are used to test the effect of delivery independent of the transcript. EchoMind is grounded in an empathy-oriented framework spanning 3 coarse and 12 fine-grained dimensions, encompassing 39 vocal attributes, and evaluated using both objective and subjective metrics. Testing 12 advanced SLMs reveals that even state-of-the-art models struggle with high-expressive vocal cues, limiting empathetic response quality. Analyses of prompt strength, speech source, and ideal vocal cue recognition reveal persistent weaknesses in instruction-following, resilience to natural speech variability, and effective use of vocal cues for empathy. These results underscore the need for SLMs that integrate linguistic content with diverse vocal cues to achieve truly empathetic conversational ability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Speech Language Models, Spoken Language Understanding, Vocal Cue Perception, Empathetic Dialogue, Benchmark Evaluation; Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22758","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:22:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:22:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-06T03:26:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing-Xuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing-Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Genshun","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Genshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqing","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Duo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen-Hua","familyName":"Ling","name":"Ling, Zhen-Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adapting Speech Foundation Models for Unified Multimodal Speech Recognition with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:36:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T23:53:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:17:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While speech foundation models (SFMs) have demonstrated remarkable performance in audio-only tasks, their adaptation to multimodal scenarios remains underexplored. This work presents UASR-LLM, a novel framework that adapts frozen SFMs to unified visual speech recognition (VSR), automatic speech recognition (ASR), and audio-visual speech recognition (AVSR) by leveraging large language models (LLMs) as text decoders. Visual representations are injected into multiple SFM layers via visual injection modules, enabling multimodal fusion and unified representation learning. The augmented SFMs are connected to decoder-only LLMs through a feed-forward adaptor, where concatenated representations and instruction prompts guide transcription. We propose a two-stage training strategy consisting of visual injection pretraining followed by speech recognition finetuning. The pretraining stage aligns audio, visual, and audio-visual representations within the frozen SFM backbone, while the finetuning stage integrates LLMs for unified optimization across speech recognition tasks. Experimental results demonstrate superior performance over state-of-the-art baselines across VSR, ASR, and AVSR under both clean and noisy conditions. Ablation studies further confirm generalization across various SFMs and LLMs, validating the effectiveness of the proposed training strategy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:41Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:47:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23006","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23006","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23006","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenran","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shenran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy Tin-Long","familyName":"Tse","name":"Tse, Timothy Tin-Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding In-Context Learning Beyond Transformers: An Investigation of State Space and Hybrid Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T04:49:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:22:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T21:09:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We perform in-depth evaluations of in-context learning (ICL) on state-of-the-art transformer, state-space, and hybrid large language models over two categories of knowledge-based ICL tasks. Using a combination of behavioral probing and intervention-based methods, we have discovered that, while LLMs of different architectures can behave similarly in task performance, their internals could remain different. We discover that function vectors (FVs) responsible for ICL are primarily located in the self-attention and Mamba layers, and speculate that Mamba2 uses a different mechanism from FVs to perform ICL. FVs are more important for ICL involving parametric knowledge retrieval, but not for contextual knowledge understanding. Our work contributes to a more nuanced understanding across architectures and task types. Methodologically, our approach also highlights the importance of combining both behavioural and mechanistic analyses to investigate LLM capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23006","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:28:46Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:28:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:07:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22747","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22747","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22747","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eeham","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Eeham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Firas","familyName":"Saidani","name":"Saidani, Firas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Van Esbroeck","name":"Van Esbroeck, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Khoury","name":"Khoury, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leila","familyName":"Kosseim","name":"Kosseim, Leila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Low-Resource Dialect Adaptation of Large Language Models: A French Dialect Case-Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T16:49:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T21:11:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:06:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T00:44:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:11:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the widespread adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs), their strongest capabilities remain largely confined to a small number of high-resource languages for which there is abundant training data. Recently, continual pre-training (CPT) has emerged as a means to fine-tune these models to low-resource regional dialects. In this paper, we study the use of CPT for dialect learning under tight data and compute budgets. Using low-rank adaptation (LoRA) and compute-efficient continual pre-training, we adapt three LLMs to the Québec French dialect using a very small dataset and benchmark them on the COLE suite. Our experiments demonstrate an improvement on the minority dialect benchmarks with minimal regression on the prestige language benchmarks with around 1% of model parameters updated. Analysis of the results demonstrate that gains are highly contingent on corpus composition. These findings indicate that CPT with parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) can narrow the dialect gap by providing cost-effective and sustainable language resource creation, expanding high-quality LLM access to minority linguistic communities. To support reproducibility and broaden access, we release the first Québec French LLMs on Hugging Face."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at LREC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22747","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:22:38Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:22:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:07:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22389","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22389","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22389","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"Thelwall","name":"Thelwall, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Small and Reasoning Large Language Models Score Journal Articles for Research Quality and Do Averaging and Few-shot Help?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T18:12:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:41:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T13:09:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:38:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Previous research has shown that journal article quality ratings from the cloud based Large Language Model (LLM) families ChatGPT and Gemini and the medium sized open weights LLM Gemma3 27b correlate moderately with expert research quality scores. This article assesses whether other medium sized LLMs, smaller LLMs, and reasoning models have similar abilities. This is tested with Gemma3 variants, Llama4 Scout, Qwen3, Magistral Small and DeepSeek R1 on a dataset of 2,780 medical, health and life science papers in 6 fields, with two different gold standards, one novel. Few-shot and score averaging approaches are also evaluated. The results suggest that medium-sized LLMs have similar performance to ChatGPT 4o-mini and Gemini 2.0 Flash, but that 1b parameters may often, and 4b sometimes, be too few. Reasoning models did not have a clear advantage. Moreover, averaging scores from multiple identical queries seems to be a universally successful strategy, and there is weak evidence that few-shot prompts (four examples) tend to help. Overall, the results show, for the first time, that smaller LLMs \u0026gt;4b have a substantial capability to rate journal articles for research quality, especially if score averaging is used, but that reasoning does not give an advantage for this task; it is therefore not recommended because it is slow. The use of LLMs to support research evaluation is now more credible since multiple variants have a similar ability, including many that can be deployed offline in a secure environment without substantial computing resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Thelwall, M. \u0026amp; Mohammadi, E. (2026). Can small and reasoning Large Language Models score journal articles for research quality and do averaging and few-shot help? Scientometrics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22389","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:14:13Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:14:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-18T02:57:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22650","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22650","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22650","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tharun","familyName":"Anand","name":"Anand, Tharun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Hassan","familyName":"Vali","name":"Vali, Mohammad Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arno","familyName":"Solin","name":"Solin, Arno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Green","familyName":"Rosh","name":"Rosh, Green","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"BH Pawan","familyName":"Prasad","name":"Prasad, BH Pawan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Attention Decomposition For Training Free Diffusion Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T12:22:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:59:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-12T16:23:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T02:00:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models achieve remarkable fidelity in image synthesis, yet precise control over their outputs for targeted editing remains challenging. A key step toward controllability is to identify interpretable directions in the model's latent representations that correspond to semantic attributes. Existing approaches for finding interpretable directions typically rely on sampling large sets of images or training auxiliary networks, which limits efficiency. We propose an analytical method that derives semantic editing directions directly from the pretrained parameters of diffusion models, requiring neither additional data nor fine-tuning. Our insight is that self-attention weight matrices encode rich structural information about the data distribution learned during training. By computing the eigenvectors of these weight matrices, we obtain robust and interpretable editing directions. Experiments demonstrate that our method produces high-quality edits across multiple datasets while reducing editing time significantly by 60% over current benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICASSP 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22650","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:20:21Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:20:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22936","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22936","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22936","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mouxiao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Mouxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Borui","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Borui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehua","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Dehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Hailin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PPE: Positional Preservation Embedding for Token Compression in Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T02:40:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:17:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T08:25:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:32:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have achieved strong performance on vision-language tasks, yet often suffer from inefficiencies due to redundant visual tokens. Existing token merging methods reduce sequence length but frequently disrupt spatial layouts and temporal continuity by disregarding positional relationships. In this work, we propose a novel encoding operator dubbed as \\textbf{P}ositional \\textbf{P}reservation \\textbf{E}mbedding (\\textbf{PPE}), which has the main hallmark of preservation of spatiotemporal structure during visual token compression. PPE explicitly introduces the disentangled encoding of 3D positions in the token dimension, enabling each compressed token to encapsulate different positions from multiple original tokens. Furthermore, we show that PPE can effectively support cascade clustering -- a progressive token compression strategy that leads to better performance retention. PPE is a parameter-free and generic operator that can be seamlessly integrated into existing token merging methods without any adjustments. Applied to state-of-the-art token merging framework, PPE achieves consistent improvements of $2\\%\\sim5\\%$ across multiple vision-language benchmarks, including MMBench (general vision understanding), TextVQA (layout understanding) and VideoMME (temporal understanding). These results demonstrate that preserving positional cues is critical for efficient and effective MLLM reasoning. Our code is available at https://github.com/MouxiaoHuang/PPE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22936","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:05Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:22:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22926","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22926","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22926","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaisheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Huaisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihui","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yige","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yige","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhimeng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangfan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hangfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasant","familyName":"Honavar","name":"Honavar, Vasant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simple Denoising Diffusion Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T02:05:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:16:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-03T14:28:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T02:04:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent Uniform State Diffusion Models (USDMs), initialized from a uniform prior, offer the promise of fast text generation due to their inherent self-correction ability compared to masked diffusion models. However, they still rely on complex loss formulations with additional computational overhead, which hinders scalability. In this work, we explore a simplified denoising-based loss for USDMs that optimizes only noise-replaced tokens, stabilizing training while matching the performance of prior methods with more complex objectives. In addition, we introduce an efficient regularization term to mitigate corruption toward uniform output distributions, which further improves performance. We demonstrate the effectiveness and efficiency of our simple and improved loss formulations by pretraining models on widely used text datasets for USDMs. More importantly, our conclusions scale to larger models, showing strong potential for large-scale training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22926","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:26:51Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:26:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:42:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22042","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22042","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22042","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Reichman","name":"Reichman, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adar","familyName":"Avsian","name":"Avsian, Adar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Larry","familyName":"Heck","name":"Heck, Larry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emotions Where Art Thou: Understanding and Characterizing the Emotional Latent Space of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T21:54:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:14:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T02:14:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:17:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work investigates how large language models (LLMs) internally represent emotion by analyzing the geometry of their hidden-state space. The paper identifies a low-dimensional emotional manifold and shows that emotional representations are directionally encoded, distributed across layers, and aligned with interpretable dimensions. These structures are stable across depth and generalize to eight real-world emotion datasets spanning five languages. Cross-domain alignment yields low error and strong linear probe performance, indicating a universal emotional subspace. Within this space, internal emotion perception can be steered while preserving semantics using a learned intervention module, with especially strong control for basic emotions across languages. These findings reveal a consistent and manipulable affective geometry in LLMs and offer insight into how they internalize and process emotion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22042","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:05:55Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:05:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:26:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Anand","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umberto","familyName":"Cappellazzo","name":"Cappellazzo, Umberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stavros","familyName":"Petridis","name":"Petridis, Stavros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maja","familyName":"Pantic","name":"Pantic, Maja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Attention Sinks and Massive Activations in Audio-Visual Speech Recognition with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T09:44:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:56:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-02T11:33:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:55:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T16:14:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-28T02:02:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have recently advanced auditory speech recognition (ASR), visual speech recognition (VSR), and audio-visual speech recognition (AVSR). However, understanding of their internal dynamics under fine-tuning remains limited. In natural language processing, recent work has revealed attention sinks, tokens that attract disproportionately high attention, and associated massive activations in which some features of sink tokens exhibit huge activation in LLMs. In this work, we are the first to study these phenomena in multimodal speech recognition. Through a detailed analysis of audio-visual LLMs, we identify attention sinks and massive activations not only at the BOS token but also at intermediate low-semantic tokens across ASR, VSR, and AVSR. We show that massive activations originate in the MLP layers and correspond to fixed feature indices across all sink tokens. We further show that intermediate sink tokens exhibit high cosine similarity to the BOS token, thereby amplifying attention and activation. Building on these insights, we introduce a simple decorrelation loss that reduces cosine similarity between BOS and other tokens, effectively mitigating intermediate sinks and massive activations. Furthermore, our method improves word error rate (WER) under high audio-visual feature downsampling while remaining stable at lower downsampling rates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE ICASSP 2026. The code is available at https://github.com/umbertocappellazzo/Llama-AVSR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:19:14Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:19:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-28T03:24:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyubeum","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Gyubeum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yemo","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Yemo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay Krishna","familyName":"Madisetti","name":"Madisetti, Vijay Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SCoPE VLM: Selective Context Processing for Efficient Document Navigation in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T17:47:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T16:39:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T02:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding long-context visual information remains a fundamental challenge for vision-language models, particularly in agentic tasks such as GUI control and web navigation. While web pages and GUI environments are inherently structured documents, current VLMs typically neglect decision-oriented document understanding in their training objectives. Existing approaches primarily extend visual embeddings to process long, high-resolution inputs, but these methods are memory-intensive and impractical for locally deployable solutions. To address these issues, we propose SCoPE VLM, a document navigation expert that leverages a novel Chain of Scroll mechanism to selectively and recursively navigate documents, focusing exclusively on relevant segments. We introduce a dedicated data generation pipeline to construct informative Chain of Scroll trajectories and Episodic Group Relative Policy Optimization, a tailored reinforcement learning method to bridge the gap between training and inference. Our method substantially reduces memory usage and effectively models human-like reading behaviors. To the best of our knowledge, SCoPE VLM is the first framework to explicitly model agentic reading patterns in multi-page document question answering, advancing the capabilities of multimodal agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:25Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-28T03:24:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Tayyab","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Muhammad Tayyab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zane","familyName":"Yong","name":"Yong, Zane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lequn","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lequn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhe","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Wenhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas Yew Jin","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Nicholas Yew Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung Ki","familyName":"Moon","name":"Moon, Seung Ki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multi-Stage Hybrid Framework for Automated Interpretation of Multi-View Engineering Drawings Using Vision Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T09:07:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:04:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T11:39:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:32:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Engineering drawings are fundamental to manufacturing communication, serving as the primary medium for conveying design intent, tolerances, and production details. However, interpreting complex multi-view drawings with dense annotations remains challenging using manual methods, generic optical character recognition (OCR) systems, or traditional deep learning approaches, due to varied layouts, orientations, and mixed symbolic-textual content. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a three-stage hybrid framework for the automated interpretation of 2D multi-view engineering drawings using modern detection and vision language models (VLMs). In the first stage, YOLOv11-det performs layout segmentation to localize key regions such as views, title blocks, and notes. The second stage uses YOLOv11-obb for orientation-aware, fine-grained detection of annotations, including measures, GD\u0026amp;T symbols, and surface roughness indicators. The third stage employs two Donut-based, OCR-free VLMs for semantic content parsing: the Alphabetical VLM extracts textual and categorical information from title blocks and notes, while the Numerical VLM interprets quantitative data such as measures, GD\u0026amp;T frames, and surface roughness. Two specialized datasets were developed to ensure robustness and generalization: 1,000 drawings for layout detection and 1,406 for annotation-level training. The Alphabetical VLM achieved an overall F1 score of 0.672, while the Numerical VLM reached 0.963, demonstrating strong performance in textual and quantitative interpretation, respectively. The unified JSON output enables seamless integration with CAD and manufacturing databases, providing a scalable solution for intelligent engineering drawing analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This draft has been accepted in the 13th International Conference on Industrial Engineering and Applications (ICIEA 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:42Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-26T02:50:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dogyun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Dogyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moayed","familyName":"Haji-Ali","name":"Haji-Ali, Moayed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Willi","familyName":"Menapace","name":"Menapace, Willi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Tulyakov","name":"Tulyakov, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwoo J.","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunwoo J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aliaksandr","familyName":"Siarohin","name":"Siarohin, Aliaksandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anil","familyName":"Kag","name":"Kag, Anil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sprint: Sparse-Dense Residual Fusion for Efficient Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T19:29:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:10:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:58:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-12T01:11:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) deliver state-of-the-art generative performance but their quadratic training cost with sequence length makes large-scale pretraining prohibitively expensive. Token dropping can reduce training cost, yet naïve strategies degrade representations, and existing methods are either parameter-heavy or fail at high drop ratios. We present SPRINT, Sparse--Dense Residual Fusion for Efficient Diffusion Transformers, a simple method that enables aggressive token dropping (up to 75%) while preserving quality. SPRINT leverages the complementary roles of shallow and deep layers: early layers process all tokens to capture local detail, deeper layers operate on a sparse subset to cut computation, and their outputs are fused through residual connections. Training follows a two-stage schedule: long masked pre-training for efficiency followed by short full-token fine-tuning to close the train--inference gap. On ImageNet-1K 256x256, SPRINT achieves 9.8x training savings with comparable FID/FDD, and at inference, its Path-Drop Guidance (PDG) nearly halves FLOPs while improving quality. These results establish SPRINT as a simple, effective, and general solution for efficient DiT training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:04:36Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:04:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-12T02:42:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22535","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22535","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22535","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"li","name":"li, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijie","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaowei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaheng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jiaheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OFFSIDE: Benchmarking Unlearning Misinformation in Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T05:05:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:51:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-03T06:29:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:12:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advances in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) intensify concerns about data privacy, making Machine Unlearning (MU), the selective removal of learned information, a critical necessity. However, existing MU benchmarks for MLLMs are limited by a lack of image diversity, potential inaccuracies, and insufficient evaluation scenarios, which fail to capture the complexity of real-world applications. To facilitate the development of MLLMs unlearning and alleviate the aforementioned limitations, we introduce OFFSIDE, a novel benchmark for evaluating misinformation unlearning in MLLMs based on football transfer rumors. This manually curated dataset contains 15.68K records for 80 players, providing a comprehensive framework with four test sets to assess forgetting efficacy, generalization, utility, and robustness. OFFSIDE supports advanced settings like selective unlearning and corrective relearning, and crucially, unimodal unlearning (forgetting only text data). Our extensive evaluation of multiple baselines reveals key findings: (1) Unimodal methods (erasing text-based knowledge) fail on multimodal rumors; (2) Unlearning efficacy is largely driven by catastrophic forgetting; (3) All methods struggle with \"visual rumors\" (rumors appear in the image); (4) The unlearned rumors can be easily recovered and (5) All methods are vulnerable to prompt attacks. These results expose significant vulnerabilities in current approaches, highlighting the need for more robust multimodal unlearning solutions. The code is available at https://github.com/zh121800/OFFSIDE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22535","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:17:38Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:17:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:11:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23415","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23415","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23415","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moona","familyName":"Mazher","name":"Mazher, Moona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geoff J. M.","familyName":"Parker","name":"Parker, Geoff J. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel C.","familyName":"Alexander","name":"Alexander, Daniel C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Generalisable Foundation Models for Brain MRI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T15:19:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:49:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-29T11:05:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-30T02:02:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-31T18:26:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-01T02:11:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models in artificial intelligence (AI) are transforming medical imaging by enabling general-purpose feature learning from large-scale, unlabeled datasets. In this work, we introduce BrainFound, a self-supervised foundation model for brain MRI, built by extending DINO-v2, a vision transformer originally designed for 2D natural images. BrainFound adapts DINO-v2 to model full 3D brain anatomy by incorporating volumetric information from sequential MRI slices, moving beyond conventional single-slice paradigms. It supports both single- and multimodal inputs, enabling a broad range of downstream tasks, including disease detection and image segmentation, while generalising across varied imaging protocols and clinical scenarios. We show that BrainFound consistently outperforms existing self-supervised pretraining strategies and supervised baselines, particularly in label-scarce and multi-contrast settings. By integrating information from diverse 3D MRI modalities (e.g., T1, T2, FLAIR), it enhances diagnostic accuracy and reduces dependency on extensive expert annotations. This flexibility makes BrainFound a scalable and practical solution for 3D neuroimaging pipelines, with significant potential for clinical deployment and research innovation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23415","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:38:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:38:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-01T03:39:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raihan Ahamed","familyName":"Rifat","name":"Rifat, Raihan Ahamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuyad Hasan","familyName":"Bhoyan","name":"Bhoyan, Fuyad Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Humaion Kabir","familyName":"Mehedi","name":"Mehedi, Md Humaion Kabir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Kaviul","familyName":"Hossain","name":"Hossain, Md Kaviul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Jakir","familyName":"Hossen","name":"Hossen, Md. Jakir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. F.","familyName":"Mridha","name":"Mridha, M. F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ConMatFormer: A Multi-attention and Transformer Integrated ConvNext based Deep Learning Model for Enhanced Diabetic Foot Ulcer Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T16:34:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-30T01:45:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diabetic foot ulcer (DFU) detection is a clinically significant yet challenging task due to the scarcity and variability of publicly available datasets. To solve these problems, we propose ConMatFormer, a new hybrid deep learning architecture that combines ConvNeXt blocks, multiple attention mechanisms convolutional block attention module (CBAM) and dual attention network (DANet), and transformer modules in a way that works together. This design facilitates the extraction of better local features and understanding of the global context, which allows us to model small skin patterns across different types of DFU very accurately. To address the class imbalance, we used data augmentation methods. A ConvNeXt block was used to obtain detailed local features in the initial stages. Subsequently, we compiled the model by adding a transformer module to enhance long-range dependency. This enabled us to pinpoint the DFU classes that were underrepresented or constituted minorities. Tests on the DS1 (DFUC2021) and DS2 (diabetic foot ulcer (DFU)) datasets showed that ConMatFormer outperformed state-of-the-art (SOTA) convolutional neural network (CNN) and Vision Transformer (ViT) models in terms of accuracy, reliability, and flexibility. The proposed method achieved an accuracy of 0.8961 and a precision of 0.9160 in a single experiment, which is a significant improvement over the current standards for classifying DFUs. In addition, by 4-fold cross-validation, the proposed model achieved an accuracy of 0.9755 with a standard deviation of only 0.0031. We further applied explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) methods, such as Grad-CAM, Grad-CAM++, and LIME, to consistently monitor the transparency and trustworthiness of the decision-making process.. Our findings set a new benchmark for DFU classification and provide a hybrid attention transformer framework for medical image analysis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:22:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:22:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-30T03:52:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23400","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23400","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23400","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Riggi","name":"Riggi, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Romano","name":"Romano, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Pilzer","name":"Pilzer, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"U.","familyName":"Becciani","name":"Becciani, U.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solar flare forecasting with foundational transformer models across image, video, and time-series modalities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T14:58:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:48:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T18:50:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:57:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.ascom.2025.101042","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a comparative study of transformer-based architectures for solar flare forecasting using heterogeneous data modalities, including images, video sequences, and time-series observations. Our analysis evaluates three recent foundational models - SigLIP2 for image encoding, VideoMAE for spatio-temporal video representation, and Moirai2 for multivariate time-series forecasting - applied to publicly available datasets of solar magnetograms from the SDO/HMI mission and soft X-ray fluxes acquired by GOES satellites. All models are trained and validated under consistent data splits and evaluation criteria, with the goal of assessing the strengths and limitations of transformer backbones across spatial and temporal representations of solar activity. We investigate multiple loss formulations (weighted BCE, focal, and score-oriented) and training balance strategies to mitigate class imbalance typical of flare datasets. Results show that while both SigLIP2 and VideoMAE achieve typical performance on image and video data (True Skill Statistic TSS~0.60-0.65), the time-series model Moirai2 reaches superior forecasting skill (TSS~0.74) using irradiance-based temporal evolution alone. These findings highlight the potential of pretrained transformer architectures and cross-modal learning for advancing operational space weather forecasting, paving the way toward unified multimodal models that integrate visual and temporal information."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23400","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-28T03:38:11Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:38:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-05T03:07:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22171","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22171","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22171","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erum","familyName":"Mushtaq","name":"Mushtaq, Erum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zalan","familyName":"Fabian","name":"Fabian, Zalan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yavuz Faruk","familyName":"Bakman","name":"Bakman, Yavuz Faruk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anil","familyName":"Ramakrishna","name":"Ramakrishna, Anil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Soltanolkotabi","name":"Soltanolkotabi, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salman","familyName":"Avestimehr","name":"Avestimehr, Salman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HARMONY: Hidden Activation Representations and Model Output-Aware Uncertainty Estimation for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T05:45:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:27:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-28T19:46:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:05:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Uncertainty Estimation (UE) plays a central role in quantifying the reliability of model outputs and reducing unsafe generations via selective prediction. In this regard, most existing probability-based UE approaches rely on predefined functions, aggregating token probabilities into a single UE score using heuristics such as length-normalization. However, these methods often fail to capture the complex relationships between generated tokens and struggle to identify biased probabilities often influenced by \\textbf{language priors}. Another line of research uses hidden representations of the model and trains simple MLP architectures to predict uncertainty. However, such functions often lose the intricate \\textbf{ inter-token dependencies}. While prior works show that hidden representations encode multimodal alignment signals, our work demonstrates that how these signals are processed has a significant impact on the UE performance. To effectively leverage these signals to identify inter-token dependencies, and vision-text alignment, we propose \\textbf{HARMONY} (Hidden Activation Representations and Model Output-Aware Uncertainty Estimation for Vision-Language Models), a novel UE framework that integrates generated tokens ('text'), model's uncertainty score at the output ('MaxProb'), and its internal belief on the visual understanding of the image and the generated token (captured by 'hidden representations') at token level via appropriate input mapping design and suitable architecture choice. Our experimental experiments across two open-ended VQA benchmarks (A-OKVQA, and VizWiz) and four state-of-the-art VLMs (LLaVA-7B, LLaVA-13B, InstructBLIP, and Qwen-VL) show that HARMONY consistently matches or surpasses existing approaches, achieving up to 5\\% improvement in AUROC and 9\\% in PRR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22171","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:08:58Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:08:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:48:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22830","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22830","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22830","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Princeton University"],"givenName":"Haowei","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Haowei","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Maryland, College Park"],"givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Hong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Maryland, College Park \u0026 Beijing Normal University"],"givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyi","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploration of Summarization by Generative Language Models for Automated Scoring of Long Essays"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T20:59:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:10:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T14:43:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:56:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T00:58:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:13:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-19T18:59:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-20T02:05:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"BERT and its variants are extensively explored for automated scoring. However, a limit of 512 tokens for these encoder-based models showed the deficiency in automated scoring of long essays. Thus, this research explores generative language models for automated scoring of long essays via summarization and prompting. The results revealed great improvement of scoring accuracy with QWK increased from 0.822 to 0.8878 for the Learning Agency Lab Automated Essay Scoring 2.0 dataset."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 5 Tables 7 Figures, Presentation at Artificial Intelligence in Measurement and Education Conference (AIME-Con)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22830","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:24:36Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:24:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-20T03:15:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22026","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22026","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22026","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Karagodin","name":"Karagodin, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yury","familyName":"Polyanskiy","name":"Polyanskiy, Yury","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe","familyName":"Rigollet","name":"Rigollet, Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Normalization in Attention Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T07, 35Q68, 37C10, 37N99, 82C22","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T21:10:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:13:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-11T18:40:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-12T02:04:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the effect of normalization schemes on token representations in deep transformers. Modeling their evolution as interacting particles on the sphere, we show that normalization acts as a form of speed regulation. This perspective enables a unified analysis of several schemes -- including Post-LN, Pre-LN, Mix-LN, Peri-LN, nGPT -- revealing how they influence clustering dynamics and representation collapse. Our framework clarifies how different schemes shape token representations across layers and provides a principled basis for comparing them, identifying Peri-LN as a particularly effective choice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025), 23 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22026","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:05:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:05:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-12T03:31:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22990","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22990","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22990","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssef","familyName":"Megahed","name":"Megahed, Youssef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Ducharme","name":"Ducharme, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aylin","familyName":"Erman","name":"Erman, Aylin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Walker","name":"Walker, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven","familyName":"Hawken","name":"Hawken, Steven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian D. C.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Adrian D. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"USF-MAE: Ultrasound Self-Supervised Foundation Model with Masked Autoencoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T04:16:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:20:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-07T04:12:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:18:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ultrasound imaging is one of the most widely used diagnostic modalities, offering real-time, radiation-free assessment across diverse clinical domains. However, interpretation of ultrasound images remains challenging due to high noise levels, operator dependence, and limited field of view, resulting in substantial inter-observer variability. Current Deep Learning approaches are hindered by the scarcity of large labeled datasets and the domain gap between general and sonographic images, which limits the transferability of models pretrained on non-medical data. To address these challenges, we introduce the Ultrasound Self-Supervised Foundation Model with Masked Autoencoding (USF-MAE), the first large-scale self-supervised MAE framework pretrained exclusively on ultrasound data. The model was pre-trained on 370,000 2D and 3D ultrasound images curated from 46 open-source datasets, collectively termed OpenUS-46, spanning over twenty anatomical regions. This curated dataset has been made publicly available to facilitate further research and reproducibility. Using a Vision Transformer encoder-decoder architecture, USF-MAE reconstructs masked image patches, enabling it to learn rich, modality-specific representations directly from unlabeled data. The pretrained encoder was fine-tuned on three public downstream classification benchmarks: BUS-BRA (breast cancer), MMOTU-2D (ovarian tumors), and GIST514-DB (gastrointestinal stromal tumors). Across all tasks, USF-MAE consistently outperformed conventional CNN and ViT baselines, achieving F1-scores of 81.6%, 79.6%, and 82.4%, respectively. Despite not using labels during pretraining, USF-MAE approached the performance of the supervised foundation model UltraSam on breast cancer classification and surpassed it on the other tasks, demonstrating strong cross-anatomical generalization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 8 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22990","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:28:23Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:28:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-10T02:50:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21849","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21849","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21849","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André G.","familyName":"Viveiros","name":"Viveiros, André G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Fernandes","name":"Fernandes, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saul","familyName":"Santos","name":"Santos, Saul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonal","familyName":"Sannigrahi","name":"Sannigrahi, Sonal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanouil","familyName":"Zaranis","name":"Zaranis, Emmanouil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuno M.","familyName":"Guerreiro","name":"Guerreiro, Nuno M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Farajian","name":"Farajian, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Colombo","name":"Colombo, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Graham","familyName":"Neubig","name":"Neubig, Graham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André F. T.","familyName":"Martins","name":"Martins, André F. T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TowerVision: Understanding and Improving Multilinguality in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.10; I.5.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T45, 68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T17:02:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T19:23:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:07:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T11:09:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:37:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite significant advances in vision-language models (VLMs), most existing work follows an English-centric design process, limiting their effectiveness in multilingual settings. In this work, we provide a comprehensive empirical study analyzing the impact of several multilingual design choices, such as training data composition, encoder selection, and text backbones. The result is TowerVision, a family of open multilingual VLMs for both image-text and video-text tasks, built upon the multilingual text-only model Tower+. TowerVision achieves competitive performance on multiple multimodal multilingual benchmarks and shows particular strength in culturally grounded tasks and multimodal translation. By incorporating visual and cultural context during fine-tuning, our models surpass existing approaches trained on substantially larger datasets, as demonstrated on ALM-Bench and Multi30K (image tasks) and ViMUL-Bench (video tasks). Alongside the models, we release VisionBlocks, a high-quality, curated vision-language dataset. Our findings highlight that multilingual vision-language training data substantially improves cross-lingual generalization -- both from high-resource to underrepresented languages and vice versa -- and that instruction-tuned LLMs are not always the optimal initialization point. To support further research, we publicly release all models, data, and training recipes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures, submitted to arXiv October 2025. All models, datasets, and training code will be released at https://huggingface.co/collections/utter-project/towervision"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21849","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:24Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-07T03:07:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21908","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21908","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21908","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enabling Robust In-Context Memory and Rapid Task Adaptation in Transformers with Hebbian and Gradient-Based Plasticity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:26:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:05:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T22:10:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:09:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models display in-context learning as an emergent effect of scale, but they rely on static weights during inference. In contrast, biological systems continually adapt via synaptic plasticity. We investigate whether explicit, biologically inspired plasticity can endow Transformers with faster in-sequence adaptation. To this end, we augment decoder-only Transformers with fast-weight modules updated either by (i) a neuromodulated Hebbian rule or (ii) the gradient-based plasticity mechanism of Duan et al. (2023). Across copying, regression, and few-shot classification tasks (CIFAR-FS, Omniglot), Hebbian plasticity consistently achieves lower loss and stronger few-shot generalization, while gradient-based updates perform best on long-horizon credit assignment. When associations are short and linearly separable, static weights suffice, defining a clear boundary condition for when plasticity helps. Analysis of learned modulatory signals reveals that gradient-based rules maintain large, persistent updates, whereas Hebbian plasticity is sharply gated around salient events. Together, these results show that explicit plasticity complements attention by enabling rapid, task-specific adaptation, and clarify when different plasticity mechanisms are most effective."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21908","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:02:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:02:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:16:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22706","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22706","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22706","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyu","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Zhengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangfu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fangfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangzhou","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Fangzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yukang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushi","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Yushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Manyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IGGT: Instance-Grounded Geometry Transformer for Semantic 3D Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T14:57:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T04:16:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:29:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T03:22:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:23:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humans naturally perceive the geometric structure and semantic content of a 3D world as intertwined dimensions, enabling coherent and accurate understanding of complex scenes. However, most prior approaches prioritize training large geometry models for low-level 3D reconstruction and treat high-level spatial understanding in isolation, overlooking the crucial interplay between these two fundamental aspects of 3D-scene analysis, thereby limiting generalization and leading to poor performance in downstream 3D understanding tasks. Recent attempts have mitigated this issue by simply aligning 3D models with specific language models, thus restricting perception to the aligned model's capacity and limiting adaptability to downstream tasks. In this paper, we propose InstanceGrounded Geometry Transformer (IGGT), an end-to-end large unified transformer to unify the knowledge for both spatial reconstruction and instance-level contextual understanding. Specifically, we design a 3D-Consistent Contrastive Learning strategy that guides IGGT to encode a unified representation with geometric structures and instance-grounded clustering through only 2D visual inputs. This representation supports consistent lifting of 2D visual inputs into a coherent 3D scene with explicitly distinct object instances. To facilitate this task, we further construct InsScene-15K, a large-scale dataset with high-quality RGB images, poses, depth maps, and 3D-consistent instance-level mask annotations with a novel data curation pipeline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://github.com/lifuguan/IGGT_official"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22706","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:21:41Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:21:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-03T03:02:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23588","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23588","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23588","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangting","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Guangting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FARMER: Flow AutoRegressive Transformer over Pixels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:54:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:59:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T07:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:31:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Directly modeling the explicit likelihood of the raw data distribution is key topic in the machine learning area, which achieves the scaling successes in Large Language Models by autoregressive modeling. However, continuous AR modeling over visual pixel data suffer from extremely long sequences and high-dimensional spaces. In this paper, we present FARMER, a novel end-to-end generative framework that unifies Normalizing Flows (NF) and Autoregressive (AR) models for tractable likelihood estimation and high-quality image synthesis directly from raw pixels. FARMER employs an invertible autoregressive flow to transform images into latent sequences, whose distribution is modeled implicitly by an autoregressive model. To address the redundancy and complexity in pixel-level modeling, we propose a self-supervised dimension reduction scheme that partitions NF latent channels into informative and redundant groups, enabling more effective and efficient AR modeling. Furthermore, we design a one-step distillation scheme to significantly accelerate inference speed and introduce a resampling-based classifier-free guidance algorithm to boost image generation quality. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FARMER achieves competitive performance compared to existing pixel-based generative models while providing exact likelihoods and scalable training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Bytedance Seed Technical Report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23588","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:42Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-31T02:12:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23252","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23252","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23252","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tawsif Tashwar","familyName":"Dipto","name":"Dipto, Tawsif Tashwar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Azmol","familyName":"Hossain","name":"Hossain, Azmol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rubayet Sabbir","familyName":"Faruque","name":"Faruque, Rubayet Sabbir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Rezuwan","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Md. Rezuwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kanij","familyName":"Fatema","name":"Fatema, Kanij","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmoy","familyName":"Shome","name":"Shome, Tanmoy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruwad","familyName":"Naswan","name":"Naswan, Ruwad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Foriduzzaman","familyName":"Zihad","name":"Zihad, Md. Foriduzzaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohaymen Ul","familyName":"Anam","name":"Anam, Mohaymen Ul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nazia","familyName":"Tasnim","name":"Tasnim, Nazia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hasan","familyName":"Mahmud","name":"Mahmud, Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Kamrul","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Md Kamrul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Mehedi Hasan","familyName":"Shawon","name":"Shawon, Md. Mehedi Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farig","familyName":"Sadeque","name":"Sadeque, Farig","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tahsin","familyName":"Reasat","name":"Reasat, Tahsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are ASR foundation models generalized enough to capture features of regional dialects for low-resource languages?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:39:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T09:41:26Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; The manuscript has to be withdrawn to address an authorship and intellectual property clarification"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:38:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional research on speech recognition modeling relies on the canonical form for most low-resource languages while automatic speech recognition (ASR) for regional dialects is treated as a fine-tuning task. To investigate the effects of dialectal variations on ASR we develop a 78-hour annotated Bengali Speech-to-Text (STT) corpus named Ben-10. Investigation from linguistic and data-driven perspectives shows that speech foundation models struggle heavily in regional dialect ASR, both in zero-shot and fine-tuned settings. We observe that all deep learning methods struggle to model speech data under dialectal variations but dialect specific model training alleviates the issue. Our dataset also serves as a out of-distribution (OOD) resource for ASR modeling under constrained resources in ASR algorithms. The dataset and code developed for this project are publicly available"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The manuscript has to be withdrawn to address an authorship and intellectual property clarification"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23252","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:34:40Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:34:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:20:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22931","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22931","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22931","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Uncertainty Quantification for Self-Evolving Large Language Models via Continual Domain Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T02:15:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:16:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T15:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T01:00:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continual Learning (CL) is essential for enabling self-evolving large language models (LLMs) to adapt and remain effective amid rapid knowledge growth. Yet, despite its importance, little attention has been given to establishing statistical reliability guarantees for LLMs under CL, particularly in the setting of continual domain pretraining (CDP). Conformal Prediction (CP) has shown promise in offering correctness guarantees for LLMs, but it faces major challenges in CDP: testing data often stems from unknown or shifting domain distributions, under which CP may no longer provide valid guarantees. Moreover, when high coverage is required, CP can yield excessively large prediction sets for unanswerable queries, reducing informativeness. To address these challenges, we introduce an adaptive rejection and non-exchangeable CP framework. Our method first estimates the distribution of questions across domains in the test set using transformer-based clustering, then reweights or resamples the calibration data accordingly. Building on this, adaptive rejection CP allows the LLM to selectively abstain from answering when its confidence or competence shifts significantly. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our framework enhances both the effectiveness and reliability of CP under CDP scenarios. Our code is available at: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/CPCL-8C12/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22931","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:26:58Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:26:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:26:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23585","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23585","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23585","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Ramos","name":"Ramos, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiram","familyName":"Calvo","name":"Calvo, Hiram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olga","familyName":"Kolesnikova","name":"Kolesnikova, Olga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hope Speech Detection in Social Media English Corpora: Performance of Traditional and Transformer Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:53:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:59:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The identification of hope speech has become a promised NLP task, considering the need to detect motivational expressions of agency and goal-directed behaviour on social media platforms. This proposal evaluates traditional machine learning models and fine-tuned transformers for a previously split hope speech dataset as train, development and test set. On development test, a linear-kernel SVM and logistic regression both reached a macro-F1 of 0.78; SVM with RBF kernel reached 0.77, and Naïve Bayes hit 0.75. Transformer models delivered better results, the best model achieved weighted precision of 0.82, weighted recall of 0.80, weighted F1 of 0.79, macro F1 of 0.79, and 0.80 accuracy. These results suggest that while optimally configured traditional machine learning models remain agile, transformer architectures detect some subtle semantics of hope to achieve higher precision and recall in hope speech detection, suggesting that larges transformers and LLMs could perform better in small datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23585","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:37Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:42:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23558","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23558","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23558","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hankun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hankun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ISA-Bench: Benchmarking Instruction Sensitivity for Large Audio Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:31:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:57:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Audio Language Models (LALMs), which couple acoustic perception with large language models (LLMs) to extract and understand diverse information from audio, have attracted intense interest from both academic and industrial communities. However, existing LALMs are highly sensitive to how instructions are phrased, affecting both (i) instruction-following rates and (ii) task performance. Yet, no existing benchmarks offer a systematic and comprehensive evaluation of this sensitivity. We introduce ISA-Bench, a dynamic benchmark evaluating instruction sensitivity for LALMs along three axes: instruction description, output format, and task composition. We assess recent open-source and proprietary LALMs using ISA-Bench, profiling both compliance and accuracy under controlled instruction variations. Experimental results reveal that even state-of-the-art LALMs suffer significant instruction sensitivity, leading to degraded performance on fundamental audio understanding tasks. To mitigate this issue, we fine-tune Qwen2-Audio on a specifically constructed complex instruction-variant dataset, achieving a marked improvement in instruction-following performance. However, this also induces nontrivial catastrophic forgetting: the model loses some previously mastered task capabilities when exposed to new instruction styles. Our benchmark provides a standardized basis for assessing and improving instruction sensitivity in LALMs, underscoring the need for instruction-robust audio understanding in real-world pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"submitted to icassp 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23558","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:41:58Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:41:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:41:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23554","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23554","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23554","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Sahay","name":"Sahay, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radhika","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Radhika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A U-Net and Transformer Pipeline for Multilingual Image Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:28:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:57:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents an end-to-end multilingual translation pipeline that integrates a custom U-Net for text detection, the Tesseract engine for text recognition, and a from-scratch sequence-to-sequence (Seq2Seq) Transformer for Neural Machine Translation (NMT). Our approach first utilizes a U-Net model, trained on a synthetic dataset , to accurately segment and detect text regions from an image. These detected regions are then processed by Tesseract to extract the source text. This extracted text is fed into a custom Transformer model trained from scratch on a multilingual parallel corpus spanning 5 languages. Unlike systems reliant on monolithic pre-trained models, our architecture emphasizes full customization and adaptability. The system is evaluated on its text detection accuracy, text recognition quality, and translation performance via BLEU scores. The complete pipeline demonstrates promising results, validating the viability of a custom-built system for translating text directly from images."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures, 5 tables, and 2 algorithms. Prepared in IEEE double-column format"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23554","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:41:52Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:41:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:41:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23464","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23464","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23464","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikesh","familyName":"Gyawali","name":"Gyawali, Nikesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doina","familyName":"Caragea","name":"Caragea, Doina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Vasenkov","name":"Vasenkov, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cornelia","familyName":"Caragea","name":"Caragea, Cornelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Large Language Models for Stance Detection on Financial Targets from SEC Filing Reports and Earnings Call Transcripts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:03:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial narratives from U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) filing reports and quarterly earnings call transcripts (ECTs) are very important for investors, auditors, and regulators. However, their length, financial jargon, and nuanced language make fine-grained analysis difficult. Prior sentiment analysis in the financial domain required a large, expensive labeled dataset, making the sentence-level stance towards specific financial targets challenging. In this work, we introduce a sentence-level corpus for stance detection focused on three core financial metrics: debt, earnings per share (EPS), and sales. The sentences were extracted from Form 10-K annual reports and ECTs, and labeled for stance (positive, negative, neutral) using the advanced ChatGPT-o3-pro model under rigorous human validation. Using this corpus, we conduct a systematic evaluation of modern large language models (LLMs) using zero-shot, few-shot, and Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting strategies. Our results show that few-shot with CoT prompting performs best compared to supervised baselines, and LLMs' performance varies across the SEC and ECT datasets. Our findings highlight the practical viability of leveraging LLMs for target-specific stance in the financial domain without requiring extensive labeled data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23464","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:39:42Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:39:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:39:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23357","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23357","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23357","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaohan","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Shaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guohui","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Guohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edmond Q.","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Edmond Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon X.","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Simon X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changchun","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Changchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large language model-based task planning for service robots: A review"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T14:06:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:45:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) and robotics, service robots are increasingly becoming an integral part of daily life, offering a wide range of services in complex environments. To deliver these services intelligently and efficiently, robust and accurate task planning capabilities are essential. This paper presents a comprehensive overview of the integration of LLMs into service robotics, with a particular focus on their role in enhancing robotic task planning. First, the development and foundational techniques of LLMs, including pre-training, fine-tuning, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and prompt engineering, are reviewed. We then explore the application of LLMs as the cognitive core-`brain'-of service robots, discussing how LLMs contribute to improved autonomy and decision-making. Furthermore, recent advancements in LLM-driven task planning across various input modalities are analyzed, including text, visual, audio, and multimodal inputs. Finally, we summarize key challenges and limitations in current research and propose future directions to advance the task planning capabilities of service robots in complex, unstructured domestic environments. This review aims to serve as a valuable reference for researchers and practitioners in the fields of artificial intelligence and robotics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to Biomimetic Intelligence and Robotics for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23357","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:37:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:37:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:37:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23334","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23334","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23334","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Atif","familyName":"Quamar","name":"Quamar, Mohammad Atif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Areeb","name":"Areeb, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishant","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Nishant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ananth","familyName":"Shreekumar","name":"Shreekumar, Ananth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Rosenthal","name":"Rosenthal, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muslum Ozgur","familyName":"Ozmen","name":"Ozmen, Muslum Ozgur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikhail","familyName":"Kuznetsov","name":"Kuznetsov, Mikhail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z. Berkay","familyName":"Celik","name":"Celik, Z. Berkay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Blockwise Search: Inference-Time Alignment for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T13:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:44:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM alignment remains a critical challenge. Inference-time methods provide a flexible alternative to fine-tuning, but their uniform computational effort often yields suboptimal alignment. We hypothesize that for many alignment tasks, the initial tokens of a response are disproportionately more critical. To leverage this principle, we introduce AdaSearch, a novel blockwise search strategy. It adaptively allocates a fixed computational budget using a sampling schedule, focusing search effort on these critical tokens. We apply AdaSearch to sequential decoding and introduce its tree-search counterpart, AdaBeam. Our comprehensive evaluation across eight LLMs demonstrates that AdaSearch outperforms strong Best-of-N and fine-tuning baselines. Specifically, win-rates improve by over 10% for harmlessness generation, controlled sentiment generation, and for mathematical reasoning tasks relative to Best-of-N."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23334","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:36:37Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:36:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:36:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23271","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23271","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23271","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed","familyName":"Aljafari","name":"Aljafari, Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ismail","familyName":"Alturki","name":"Alturki, Ismail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Mori","name":"Mori, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yehya","familyName":"Kadumi","name":"Kadumi, Yehya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mubeen AI: A Specialized Arabic Language Model for Heritage Preservation and User Intent Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6; I.2.0; H.3.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50 (68T50 Natural language processing)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:29:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:40:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mubeen is a proprietary Arabic language model developed by MASARAT SA, optimized for deep understanding of Arabic linguistics, Islamic studies, and cultural heritage. Trained on an extensive collection of authentic Arabic sources significantly expanded by digitizing historical manuscripts via a proprietary Arabic OCR engine, the model incorporates seminal scholarly works in linguistics, jurisprudence, hadith, and Quranic exegesis, alongside thousands of academic theses and peer-reviewed research papers. Conditioned through a deep linguistic engineering framework, Mubeen masters not just the meaning but the eloquence of Arabic, enabling precise understanding across classical texts, contemporary writing, and regional dialects with focus on comprehending user intent and delivering accurate, contextually relevant responses. Unlike other Arabic models relying on translated English data that often fail in intent detection or retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), Mubeen uses native Arabic sources to ensure cultural authenticity and accuracy. Its core innovation is the Practical Closure Architecture, designed to solve the \"Utility Gap Crisis\" where factually correct answers fail to resolve users' core needs, forcing them into frustrating cycles of re-prompting. By prioritizing clarity and decisive guidance, Mubeen transforms from an information repository into a decisive guide, aligning with Saudi Vision 2030. The model's architecture combines deep heritage specialization with multi-disciplinary expert modules, enabling robust performance across both cultural preservation and general knowledge domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 2 figures, 3 tables. Includes appendices on ethical guidelines and training framework. Submitted September 04, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23271","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:35:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:35:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:35:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Young Min","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Young Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finding 3D Scene Analogies with Multimodal Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T10:23:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:34:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Connecting current observations with prior experiences helps robots adapt and plan in new, unseen 3D environments. Recently, 3D scene analogies have been proposed to connect two 3D scenes, which are smooth maps that align scene regions with common spatial relationships. These maps enable detailed transfer of trajectories or waypoints, potentially supporting demonstration transfer for imitation learning or task plan transfer across scenes. However, existing methods for the task require additional training and fixed object vocabularies. In this work, we propose to use multimodal foundation models for finding 3D scene analogies in a zero-shot, open-vocabulary setting. Central to our approach is a hybrid neural representation of scenes that consists of a sparse graph based on vision-language model features and a feature field derived from 3D shape foundation models. 3D scene analogies are then found in a coarse-to-fine manner, by first aligning the graph and refining the correspondence with feature fields. Our method can establish accurate correspondences between complex scenes, and we showcase applications in trajectory and waypoint transfer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to FM4RoboPlan workshop at RSS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:33:03Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:33:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:33:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SI-Bench: Benchmarking Social Intelligence of Large Language Models in Human-to-Human Conversations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T10:21:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) develop anthropomorphic abilities, they are increasingly being deployed as autonomous agents to interact with humans. However, evaluating their performance in realistic and complex social interactions remains a significant challenge. Most previous research built datasets through simulated agent-to-agent interactions, which fails to capture the authentic linguistic styles and relational dynamics found in real human conversations. To address this gap, we introduce SI-Bench, a novel benchmark designed to evaluate aspects of social intelligence in LLMs. Grounded in broad social science theories, SI-Bench contains 2,221 authentic multi-turn dialogues collected from a social networking application. We further selected a subset of 312 dialogues for manual annotation across 8 major models. The experiments show that SOTA models have surpassed the human expert in process reasoning under complex social situations, yet they still fall behind humans in reply quality. Moreover, introducing Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning may degrade the performance of LLMs in social dialogue tasks. All datasets are openly available at https://github.com/SI-Bench/SI-Bench.git."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:33:00Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:33:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:33:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas M.","familyName":"Moerland","name":"Moerland, Thomas M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"Preuss","name":"Preuss, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aske","familyName":"Plaat","name":"Plaat, Aske","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"François-Lavet","name":"François-Lavet, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward S.","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Edward S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Guiding Skill Discovery with Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T09:47:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:33:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning diverse skills without hand-crafted reward functions could accelerate reinforcement learning in downstream tasks. However, existing skill discovery methods focus solely on maximizing the diversity of skills without considering human preferences, which leads to undesirable behaviors and possibly dangerous skills. For instance, a cheetah robot trained using previous methods learns to roll in all directions to maximize skill diversity, whereas we would prefer it to run without flipping or entering hazardous areas. In this work, we propose a Foundation model Guided (FoG) skill discovery method, which incorporates human intentions into skill discovery through foundation models. Specifically, FoG extracts a score function from foundation models to evaluate states based on human intentions, assigning higher values to desirable states and lower to undesirable ones. These scores are then used to re-weight the rewards of skill discovery algorithms. By optimizing the re-weighted skill discovery rewards, FoG successfully learns to eliminate undesirable behaviors, such as flipping or rolling, and to avoid hazardous areas in both state-based and pixel-based tasks. Interestingly, we show that FoG can discover skills involving behaviors that are difficult to define. Interactive visualisations are available from https://sites.google.com/view/submission-fog."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:32:38Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:32:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:32:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaoyan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiqun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Implicit Modeling for Transferability Estimation of Vision Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T09:21:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:32:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transferability estimation identifies the best pre-trained models for downstream tasks without incurring the high computational cost of full fine-tuning. This capability facilitates deployment and advances the pre-training and fine-tuning paradigm. However, existing methods often struggle to accurately assess transferability for emerging pre-trained models with diverse architectures, training strategies, and task alignments. In this work, we propose Implicit Transferability Modeling (ITM), a novel framework that implicitly models each model's intrinsic transferability, coupled with a Divide-and-Conquer Variational Approximation (DVA) strategy to efficiently approximate embedding space evolution. This design enables generalization across a broader range of models and downstream tasks. Extensive experiments on a comprehensive benchmark--spanning extensive training regimes and a wider variety of model types--demonstrate that ITM consistently outperforms existing methods in terms of stability, effectiveness, and efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:32:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:32:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:32:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23144","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23144","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23144","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DQ3D: Depth-guided Query for Transformer-Based 3D Object Detection in Traffic Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T09:20:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:32:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D object detection from multi-view images in traffic scenarios has garnered significant attention in recent years. Many existing approaches rely on object queries that are generated from 3D reference points to localize objects. However, a limitation of these methods is that some reference points are often far from the target object, which can lead to false positive detections. In this paper, we propose a depth-guided query generator for 3D object detection (DQ3D) that leverages depth information and 2D detections to ensure that reference points are sampled from the surface or interior of the object. Furthermore, to address partially occluded objects in current frame, we introduce a hybrid attention mechanism that fuses historical detection results with depth-guided queries, thereby forming hybrid queries. Evaluation on the nuScenes dataset demonstrates that our method outperforms the baseline by 6.3\\% in terms of mean Average Precision (mAP) and 4.3\\% in the NuScenes Detection Score (NDS)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23144","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:32:06Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:32:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:32:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23119","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23119","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23119","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Lin","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yi-Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhexi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Zhexi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhizhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuoyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Shi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wei-Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniDexGrasp: Generalizable Dexterous Grasping via Foundation Model and Force Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T08:48:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:29:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Enabling robots to dexterously grasp and manipulate objects based on human commands is a promising direction in robotics. However, existing approaches are challenging to generalize across diverse objects or tasks due to the limited scale of semantic dexterous grasp datasets. Foundation models offer a new way to enhance generalization, yet directly leveraging them to generate feasible robotic actions remains challenging due to the gap between abstract model knowledge and physical robot execution. To address these challenges, we propose OmniDexGrasp, a generalizable framework that achieves omni-capabilities in user prompting, dexterous embodiment, and grasping tasks by combining foundation models with the transfer and control strategies. OmniDexGrasp integrates three key modules: (i) foundation models are used to enhance generalization by generating human grasp images supporting omni-capability of user prompt and task; (ii) a human-image-to-robot-action transfer strategy converts human demonstrations into executable robot actions, enabling omni dexterous embodiment; (iii) force-aware adaptive grasp strategy ensures robust and stable grasp execution. Experiments in simulation and on real robots validate the effectiveness of OmniDexGrasp on diverse user prompts, grasp task and dexterous hands, and further results show its extensibility to dexterous manipulation tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://isee-laboratory.github.io/OmniDexGrasp/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23119","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:31:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:31:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:31:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23066","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23066","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23066","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yushuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qishuai","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Qishuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kent Chiu","familyName":"Jin-Chun","name":"Jin-Chun, Kent Chiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth Zhu","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Kenneth Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donald","familyName":"MacDonald","name":"MacDonald, Donald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Stage Field Extraction of Financial Documents with OCR and Compact Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T06:56:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:25:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial documents are essential sources of information for regulators, auditors, and financial institutions, particularly for assessing the wealth and compliance of Small and Medium-sized Businesses. However, SMB documents are often difficult to parse. They are rarely born digital and instead are distributed as scanned images that are none machine readable. The scans themselves are low in resolution, affected by skew or rotation, and often contain noisy backgrounds. These documents also tend to be heterogeneous, mixing narratives, tables, figures, and multilingual content within the same report. Such characteristics pose major challenges for automated information extraction, especially when relying on end to end large Vision Language Models, which are computationally expensive, sensitive to noise, and slow when applied to files with hundreds of pages.\n We propose a multistage pipeline that leverages traditional image processing models and OCR extraction, together with compact VLMs for structured field extraction of large-scale financial documents. Our approach begins with image pre-processing, including segmentation, orientation detection, and size normalization. Multilingual OCR is then applied to recover page-level text. Upon analyzing the text information, pages are retrieved for coherent sections. Finally, compact VLMs are operated within these narrowed-down scopes to extract structured financial indicators.\n Our approach is evaluated using an internal corpus of multi-lingual, scanned financial documents. The results demonstrate that compact VLMs, together with a multistage pipeline, achieves 8.8 times higher field level accuracy relative to directly feeding the whole document into large VLMs, only at 0.7 percent of the GPU cost and 92.6 percent less end-to-end service latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23066","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:12Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:30:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23055","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23055","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23055","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Lero, the Research Ireland Centre for Software, University of Limerick, Ireland"],"givenName":"Manjeshwar Aniruddh","familyName":"Mallya","name":"Mallya, Manjeshwar Aniruddh","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University College Dublin"],"givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Ferrari","name":"Ferrari, Alessio","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Padova, Italy"],"givenName":"Mohammad Amin","familyName":"Zadenoori","name":"Zadenoori, Mohammad Amin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Lero, the Research Ireland Centre for Software, University of Limerick, Ireland"],"givenName":"Jacek","familyName":"Dąbrowski","name":"Dąbrowski, Jacek","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Online User Feedback to Requirements: Evaluating Large Language Models for Classification and Specification Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T06:33:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:24:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"[Context and Motivation] Online user feedback provides valuable information to support requirements engineering (RE). However, analyzing online user feedback is challenging due to its large volume and noise. Large language models (LLMs) show strong potential to automate this process and outperform previous techniques. They can also enable new tasks, such as generating requirements specifications.\n [Question-Problem] Despite their potential, the use of LLMs to analyze user feedback for RE remains underexplored. Existing studies offer limited empirical evidence, lack thorough evaluation, and rarely provide replication packages, undermining validity and reproducibility.\n [Principal Idea-Results] We evaluate five lightweight open-source LLMs on three RE tasks: user request classification, NFR classification, and requirements specification generation. Classification performance was measured on two feedback datasets, and specification quality via human evaluation. LLMs achieved moderate-to-high classification accuracy (F1 ~ 0.47-0.68) and moderately high specification quality (mean ~ 3/5).\n [Contributions] We newly explore lightweight LLMs for feedback-driven requirements development. Our contributions are: (i) an empirical evaluation of lightweight LLMs on three RE tasks, (ii) a replication package, and (iii) insights into their capabilities and limitations for RE."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23055","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:29:56Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:29:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:29:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanchao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haoxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenzhong","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Zhenzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Knocking-Heads Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T06:28:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:24:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-head attention (MHA) has become the cornerstone of modern large language models, enhancing representational capacity through parallel attention heads. However, increasing the number of heads inherently weakens individual head capacity, and existing attention mechanisms - whether standard MHA or its variants like grouped-query attention (GQA) and grouped-tied attention (GTA) - simply concatenate outputs from isolated heads without strong interaction. To address this limitation, we propose knocking-heads attention (KHA), which enables attention heads to \"knock\" on each other - facilitating cross-head feature-level interactions before the scaled dot-product attention. This is achieved by applying a shared, diagonally-initialized projection matrix across all heads. The diagonal initialization preserves head-specific specialization at the start of training while allowing the model to progressively learn integrated cross-head representations. KHA adds only minimal parameters and FLOPs and can be seamlessly integrated into MHA, GQA, GTA, and other attention variants. We validate KHA by training a 6.1B parameter MoE model (1.01B activated) on 1T high-quality tokens. Compared to baseline attention mechanisms, KHA brings superior and more stable training dynamics, achieving better performance across downstream tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:29:52Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:29:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:29:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23011","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23011","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23011","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sammriddh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Sammriddh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonit","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Sonit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mira","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Mira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LangLingual: A Personalised, Exercise-oriented English Language Learning Tool Leveraging Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T05:11:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:22:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language educators strive to create a rich experience for learners, while they may be restricted in the extend of feedback and practice they can provide. We present the design and development of LangLingual, a conversational agent built using the LangChain framework and powered by Large Language Models. The system is specifically designed to provide real-time, grammar-focused feedback, generate context-aware language exercises and track learner proficiency over time. The paper discusses the architecture, implementation and evaluation of LangLingual in detail. The results indicate strong usability, positive learning outcomes and encouraging learner engagement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23011","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:28:53Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:28:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:28:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22993","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22993","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22993","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zidong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoyan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhuoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenmei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhenmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingyu","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Language Models Compose Skills In-Context?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T04:18:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:21:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Composing basic skills from simple tasks to accomplish composite tasks is crucial for modern intelligent systems. We investigate the in-context composition ability of language models to perform composite tasks that combine basic skills demonstrated in in-context examples. This is more challenging than the standard setting, where skills and their composition can be learned in training. We conduct systematic experiments on various representative open-source language models, utilizing linguistic and logical tasks designed to probe composition abilities. The results reveal that simple task examples can have a surprising negative impact on the performance, because the models generally struggle to recognize and assemble the skills correctly, even with Chain-of-Thought examples. Theoretical analysis further shows that it is crucial to align examples with the corresponding steps in the composition. This inspires a method for the probing tasks, whose improved performance provides positive support for our insights."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22993","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:28:27Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:28:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:28:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22982","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22982","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22982","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanchen","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Guanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianlong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jianlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingtong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingtong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianqing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Caiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingcao","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Qingcao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QoSGMAA: A Robust Multi-Order Graph Attention and Adversarial Framework for Sparse QoS Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.5; I.2.6; I.2.10; I.2.7; I.6.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T04:03:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:20:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of internet technologies, network services have become critical for delivering diverse and reliable applications to users. However, the exponential growth in the number of available services has resulted in many similar offerings, posing significant challenges in selecting optimal services. Predicting Quality of Service (QoS) accurately thus becomes a fundamental prerequisite for ensuring reliability and user satisfaction. However, existing QoS prediction methods often fail to capture rich contextual information and exhibit poor performance under extreme data sparsity and structural noise. To bridge this gap, we propose a novel architecture, QoSMGAA, specifically designed to enhance prediction accuracy in complex and noisy network service environments. QoSMGAA integrates a multi-order attention mechanism to aggregate extensive contextual data and predict missing QoS values effectively. Additionally, our method incorporates adversarial neural networks to perform autoregressive supervised learning based on transformed interaction matrices. To capture complex, higher-order interactions among users and services, we employ a discrete sampling technique leveraging the Gumbel-Softmax method to generate informative negative samples. Comprehensive experimental validation conducted on large-scale real-world datasets demonstrates that our proposed model significantly outperforms existing baseline methods, highlighting its strong potential for practical deployment in service selection and recommendation scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22982","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:28:11Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:28:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:28:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22964","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22964","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22964","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liling","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Liling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedram","familyName":"Ghamisi","name":"Ghamisi, Pedram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Plaza","name":"Plaza, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Leyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Survey of Multimodal Geospatial Foundation Models: Techniques, Applications, and Challenges"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:40:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have transformed natural language processing and computer vision, and their impact is now reshaping remote sensing image analysis. With powerful generalization and transfer learning capabilities, they align naturally with the multimodal, multi-resolution, and multi-temporal characteristics of remote sensing data. To address unique challenges in the field, multimodal geospatial foundation models (GFMs) have emerged as a dedicated research frontier. This survey delivers a comprehensive review of multimodal GFMs from a modality-driven perspective, covering five core visual and vision-language modalities. We examine how differences in imaging physics and data representation shape interaction design, and we analyze key techniques for alignment, integration, and knowledge transfer to tackle modality heterogeneity, distribution shifts, and semantic gaps. Advances in training paradigms, architectures, and task-specific adaptation strategies are systematically assessed alongside a wealth of emerging benchmarks. Representative multimodal visual and vision-language GFMs are evaluated across ten downstream tasks, with insights into their architectures, performance, and application scenarios. Real-world case studies, spanning land cover mapping, agricultural monitoring, disaster response, climate studies, and geospatial intelligence, demonstrate the practical potential of GFMs. Finally, we outline pressing challenges in domain generalization, interpretability, efficiency, and privacy, and chart promising avenues for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22964","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:45Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:27:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22960","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22960","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22960","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangkai","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhangkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhui","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaize","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Kaize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhidong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longbing","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Longbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FAME: Fairness-aware Attention-modulated Video Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:34:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training-free video editing (VE) models tend to fall back on gender stereotypes when rendering profession-related prompts. We propose \\textbf{FAME} for \\textit{Fairness-aware Attention-modulated Video Editing} that mitigates profession-related gender biases while preserving prompt alignment and temporal consistency for coherent VE. We derive fairness embeddings from existing minority representations by softly injecting debiasing tokens into the text encoder. Simultaneously, FAME integrates fairness modulation into both temporal self attention and prompt-to-region cross attention to mitigate the motion corruption and temporal inconsistency caused by directly introducing fairness cues. For temporal self attention, FAME introduces a region constrained attention mask combined with time decay weighting, which enhances intra-region coherence while suppressing irrelevant inter-region interactions. For cross attention, it reweights tokens to region matching scores by incorporating fairness sensitive similarity masks derived from debiasing prompt embeddings. Together, these modulations keep fairness-sensitive semantics tied to the right visual regions and prevent temporal drift across frames. Extensive experiments on new VE fairness-oriented benchmark \\textit{FairVE} demonstrate that FAME achieves stronger fairness alignment and semantic fidelity, surpassing existing VE baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22960","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:40Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:27:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anwesan","familyName":"Pal","name":"Pal, Anwesan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karen","familyName":"Hovsepian","name":"Hovsepian, Karen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tinghao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Tinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengnan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Mengnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somendra","familyName":"Tripathi","name":"Tripathi, Somendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikos","familyName":"Kanakaris","name":"Kanakaris, Nikos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Mihaila","name":"Mihaila, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumit","familyName":"Nigam","name":"Nigam, Sumit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tagging-Augmented Generation: Assisting Language Models in Finding Intricate Knowledge In Long Contexts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:23:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent investigations into effective context lengths of modern flagship large language models (LLMs) have revealed major limitations in effective question answering (QA) and reasoning over long and complex contexts for even the largest and most impressive cadre of models. While approaches like retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and chunk-based re-ranking attempt to mitigate this issue, they are sensitive to chunking, embedding and retrieval strategies and models, and furthermore, rely on extensive pre-processing, knowledge acquisition and indexing steps. In this paper, we propose Tagging-Augmented Generation (TAG), a lightweight data augmentation strategy that boosts LLM performance in long-context scenarios, without degrading and altering the integrity and composition of retrieved documents. We validate our hypothesis by augmenting two challenging and directly relevant question-answering benchmarks -- NoLima and NovelQA -- and show that tagging the context or even just adding tag definitions into QA prompts leads to consistent performance gains over the baseline -- up to 17% for 32K token contexts, and 2.9% in complex reasoning question-answering for multi-hop queries requiring knowledge across a wide span of text. Additional details are available at https://sites.google.com/view/tag-emnlp."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper accepted at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:34Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:27:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianne","familyName":"Arriola","name":"Arriola, Marianne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yair","familyName":"Schiff","name":"Schiff, Yair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Phung","name":"Phung, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Gokaslan","name":"Gokaslan, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volodymyr","familyName":"Kuleshov","name":"Kuleshov, Volodymyr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Encoder-Decoder Diffusion Language Models for Efficient Training and Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T22:05:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:11:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discrete diffusion models enable parallel token sampling for faster inference than autoregressive approaches. However, prior diffusion models use a decoder-only architecture, which requires sampling algorithms that invoke the full network at every denoising step and incur high computational cost. Our key insight is that discrete diffusion models perform two types of computation: 1) representing clean tokens and 2) denoising corrupted tokens, which enables us to use separate modules for each task. We propose an encoder-decoder architecture to accelerate discrete diffusion inference, which relies on an encoder to represent clean tokens and a lightweight decoder to iteratively refine a noised sequence. We also show that this architecture enables faster training of block diffusion models, which partition sequences into blocks for better quality and are commonly used in diffusion language model inference. We introduce a framework for Efficient Encoder-Decoder Diffusion (E2D2), consisting of an architecture with specialized training and sampling algorithms, and we show that E2D2 achieves superior trade-offs between generation quality and inference throughput on summarization, translation, and mathematical reasoning tasks. We provide the code, model weights, and blog post on the project page: https://m-arriola.com/e2d2"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. We provide the code at https://github.com/kuleshov-group/e2d2"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22844","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22844","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22844","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prerna","familyName":"Ravi","name":"Ravi, Prerna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong Won","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dong Won","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beatriz","familyName":"Flamia","name":"Flamia, Beatriz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jasmine","familyName":"David","name":"David, Jasmine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon","familyName":"Hanks","name":"Hanks, Brandon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cynthia","familyName":"Breazeal","name":"Breazeal, Cynthia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grace","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Grace","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Large Language Models to Identify Conversation Threads in Collaborative Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T21:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding how ideas develop and flow in small-group conversations is critical for analyzing collaborative learning. A key structural feature of these interactions is threading, the way discourse talk naturally organizes into interwoven topical strands that evolve over time. While threading has been widely studied in asynchronous text settings, detecting threads in synchronous spoken dialogue remains challenging due to overlapping turns and implicit cues. At the same time, large language models (LLMs) show promise for automating discourse analysis but often struggle with long-context tasks that depend on tracing these conversational links. In this paper, we investigate whether explicit thread linkages can improve LLM-based coding of relational moves in group talk. We contribute a systematic guidebook for identifying threads in synchronous multi-party transcripts and benchmark different LLM prompting strategies for automated threading. We then test how threading influences performance on downstream coding of conversational analysis frameworks, that capture core collaborative actions such as agreeing, building, and eliciting. Our results show that providing clear conversational thread information improves LLM coding performance and underscores the heavy reliance of downstream analysis on well-structured dialogue. We also discuss practical trade-offs in time and cost, emphasizing where human-AI hybrid approaches can yield the best value. Together, this work advances methods for combining LLMs and robust conversational thread structures to make sense of complex, real-time group interactions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In Submission: Journal of Educational Data Mining (jEDM) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22844","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:24:55Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:24:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:24:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aya","familyName":"Nakayama","name":"Nakayama, Aya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuji","familyName":"Nishimura","name":"Nishimura, Yuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaito","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Kaito","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic-Preserving Cross-Style Visual Reasoning for Robust Multi-Modal Understanding in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T21:11:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:10:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The \"style trap\" poses a significant challenge for Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), hindering robust semantic understanding across diverse visual styles, especially in in-context learning (ICL). Existing methods often fail to effectively decouple style from content, hindering generalization. To address this, we propose the Semantic-Preserving Cross-Style Visual Reasoner (SP-CSVR), a novel framework for stable semantic understanding and adaptive cross-style visual reasoning. SP-CSVR integrates a Cross-Style Feature Encoder (CSFE) for style-content disentanglement, a Semantic-Aligned In-Context Decoder (SAICD) for efficient few-shot style adaptation, and an Adaptive Semantic Consistency Module (ASCM) employing multi-task contrastive learning to enforce cross-style semantic invariance. Extensive experiments on a challenging multi-style dataset demonstrate SP-CSVR's state-of-the-art performance across visual captioning, visual question answering, and in-context style adaptation. Comprehensive evaluations, including ablation studies and generalization analysis, confirm SP-CSVR's efficacy in enhancing robustness, generalization, and efficiency across diverse visual styles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22823","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22823","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22823","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Poli","familyName":"Nemkova","name":"Nemkova, Poli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amrit","familyName":"Adhikari","name":"Adhikari, Amrit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Pearson","name":"Pearson, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vamsi Krishna","familyName":"Sadu","name":"Sadu, Vamsi Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark V.","familyName":"Albert","name":"Albert, Mark V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Lingual Stability and Bias in Instruction-Tuned Language Models for Humanitarian NLP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T20:32:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:10:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humanitarian organizations face a critical choice: invest in costly commercial APIs or rely on free open-weight models for multilingual human rights monitoring. While commercial systems offer reliability, open-weight alternatives lack empirical validation -- especially for low-resource languages common in conflict zones. This paper presents the first systematic comparison of commercial and open-weight large language models (LLMs) for human-rights-violation detection across seven languages, quantifying the cost-reliability trade-off facing resource-constrained organizations. Across 78,000 multilingual inferences, we evaluate six models -- four instruction-aligned (Claude-Sonnet-4, DeepSeek-V3, Gemini-Flash-2.0, GPT-4.1-mini) and two open-weight (LLaMA-3-8B, Mistral-7B) -- using both standard classification metrics and new measures of cross-lingual reliability: Calibration Deviation (CD), Decision Bias (B), Language Robustness Score (LRS), and Language Stability Score (LSS). Results show that alignment, not scale, determines stability: aligned models maintain near-invariant accuracy and balanced calibration across typologically distant and low-resource languages (e.g., Lingala, Burmese), while open-weight models exhibit significant prompt-language sensitivity and calibration drift. These findings demonstrate that multilingual alignment enables language-agnostic reasoning and provide practical guidance for humanitarian organizations balancing budget constraints with reliability in multilingual deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22823","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:24:26Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:24:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:24:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22818","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22818","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22818","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soham","familyName":"Pahari","name":"Pahari, Soham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep Chand","familyName":"Kumain","name":"Kumain, Sandeep Chand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Air Quality Prediction Using LOESS-ARIMA and Multi-Scale CNN-BiLSTM with Residual-Gated Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.5.4; C.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68U35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T20:18:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:09:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Air pollution remains a critical environmental and public health concern in Indian megacities such as Delhi, Kolkata, and Mumbai, where sudden spikes in pollutant levels challenge timely intervention. Accurate Air Quality Index (AQI) forecasting is difficult due to the coexistence of linear trends, seasonal variations, and volatile nonlinear patterns. This paper proposes a hybrid forecasting framework that integrates LOESS decomposition, ARIMA modeling, and a multi-scale CNN-BiLSTM network with a residual-gated attention mechanism. The LOESS step separates the AQI series into trend, seasonal, and residual components, with ARIMA modeling the smooth components and the proposed deep learning module capturing multi-scale volatility in the residuals. Model hyperparameters are tuned via the Unified Adaptive Multi-Stage Metaheuristic Optimizer (UAMMO), combining multiple optimization strategies for efficient convergence. Experiments on 2021-2023 AQI datasets from the Central Pollution Control Board show that the proposed method consistently outperforms statistical, deep learning, and hybrid baselines across PM2.5, O3, CO, and NOx in three major cities, achieving up to 5-8% lower MSE and higher R^2 scores (\u0026gt;0.94) for all pollutants. These results demonstrate the framework's robustness, sensitivity to sudden pollution events, and applicability to urban air quality management."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22818","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:24:19Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:24:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:24:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thu Phuong","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Thu Phuong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duc M.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duc M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyotaek","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hyotaek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwook","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hyunwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunmin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Hyunmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungahn","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Sungahn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taehwan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taehwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VEHME: A Vision-Language Model For Evaluating Handwritten Mathematics Expressions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T19:03:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:08:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automatically assessing handwritten mathematical solutions is an important problem in educational technology with practical applications, but it remains a significant challenge due to the diverse formats, unstructured layouts, and symbolic complexity of student work. To address this challenge, we introduce VEHME-a Vision-Language Model for Evaluating Handwritten Mathematics Expressions-designed to assess open-form handwritten math responses with high accuracy and interpretable reasoning traces. VEHME integrates a two-phase training pipeline: (i) supervised fine-tuning using structured reasoning data, and (ii) reinforcement learning that aligns model outputs with multi-dimensional grading objectives, including correctness, reasoning depth, and error localization. To enhance spatial understanding, we propose an Expression-Aware Visual Prompting Module, trained on our synthesized multi-line math expressions dataset to robustly guide attention in visually heterogeneous inputs. Evaluated on AIHub and FERMAT datasets, VEHME achieves state-of-the-art performance among open-source models and approaches the accuracy of proprietary systems, demonstrating its potential as a scalable and accessible tool for automated math assessment. Our training and experiment code is publicly available at our GitHub repository."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025. Project Website: https://vehme.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:51Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:23:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22785","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22785","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22785","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prayag","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Prayag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Calibrated Consistency can Fight Back for Adversarial Robustness in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T18:37:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained vision-language models (VLMs) such as CLIP have demonstrated strong zero-shot capabilities across diverse domains, yet remain highly vulnerable to adversarial perturbations that disrupt image-text alignment and compromise reliability. Existing defenses typically rely on adversarial fine-tuning with labeled data, limiting their applicability in zero-shot settings. In this work, we identify two key weaknesses of current CLIP adversarial attacks -- lack of semantic guidance and vulnerability to view variations -- collectively termed semantic and viewpoint fragility. To address these challenges, we propose Self-Calibrated Consistency (SCC), an effective test-time defense. SCC consists of two complementary modules: Semantic consistency, which leverages soft pseudo-labels from counterattack warm-up and multi-view predictions to regularize cross-modal alignment and separate the target embedding from confusable negatives; and Spatial consistency, aligning perturbed visual predictions via augmented views to stabilize inference under adversarial perturbations. Together, these modules form a plug-and-play inference strategy. Extensive experiments on 22 benchmarks under diverse attack settings show that SCC consistently improves the zero-shot robustness of CLIP while maintaining accuracy, and can be seamlessly integrated with other VLMs for further gains. These findings highlight the great potential of establishing an adversarially robust paradigm from CLIP, with implications extending to broader vision-language domains such as BioMedCLIP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22785","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:33Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:23:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22751","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22751","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22751","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piyushkumar","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Piyushkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Modal Fact-Verification Framework for Reducing Hallucinations in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T16:58:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:06:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Language Models have transformed how we interact with AI systems, they suffer from a critical flaw: they confidently generate false information that sounds entirely plausible. This hallucination problem has become a major barrier to deploying these models in real-world applications where accuracy matters. We developed a fact verification framework that catches and corrects these errors in real-time by cross checking LLM outputs against multiple knowledge sources. Our system combines structured databases, live web searches, and academic literature to verify factual claims as they're generated. When we detect inconsistencies, we automatically correct them while preserving the natural flow of the response. Testing across various domains showed we could reduce hallucinations by 67% without sacrificing response quality. Domain experts in healthcare, finance, and scientific research rated our corrected outputs 89% satisfactory a significant improvement over unverified LLM responses. This work offers a practical solution for making LLMs more trustworthy in applications where getting facts wrong isn't an option."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22751","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:22:44Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:22:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:22:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22673","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22673","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22673","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hagay","familyName":"Michaeli","name":"Michaeli, Hagay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Soudry","name":"Soudry, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Alias-Free ViT: Fractional Shift Invariance via Linear Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T13:28:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have emerged as a competitive alternative to convnets in vision tasks, yet they lack the architectural inductive bias of convnets, which may hinder their potential performance. Specifically, Vision Transformers (ViTs) are not translation-invariant and are more sensitive to minor image translations than standard convnets. Previous studies have shown, however, that convnets are also not perfectly shift-invariant, due to aliasing in downsampling and nonlinear layers. Consequently, anti-aliasing approaches have been proposed to certify convnets' translation robustness. Building on this line of work, we propose an Alias-Free ViT, which combines two main components. First, it uses alias-free downsampling and nonlinearities. Second, it uses linear cross-covariance attention that is shift-equivariant to both integer and fractional translations, enabling a shift-invariant global representation. Our model maintains competitive performance in image classification and outperforms similar-sized models in terms of robustness to adversarial translations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025. Code is available at https://github.com/hmichaeli/alias_free_vit"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22673","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:20:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:20:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:20:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divya Jyoti","familyName":"Bajpai","name":"Bajpai, Divya Jyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manjesh Kumar","familyName":"Hanawal","name":"Hanawal, Manjesh Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FastVLM: Self-Speculative Decoding for Fast Vision-Language Model Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T11:49:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:59:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language Models (VLMs) have made significant strides in visual understanding and query response generation, but often face challenges of high computational cost and inference latency due to autoregressive decoding. In this work, we introduce an imitation-learning-based Self-Speculative Decoding (SSD) framework, named FastVLM, to address these limitations. Our approach employs a lightweight draft model for token generation in an autoregressive manner, while a full model verifies these tokens non-autoregressively. Accepted tokens proceed seamlessly, while rejected tokens are corrected by the full model and used to guide the draft model's refinement. Through an imitation network, FastVLM enhances the draft model by integrating deeper level insights from the full model's architecture. Also, it maintains the performance integrity of the full model while training the draft model, achieving a balance between efficiency and accuracy. Our method speeds up the inference process by 1.55-1.85x as compared to the final layer with minimal loss in performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for presentation at the main Conference IJCNLP-AACL 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22614","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22614","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22614","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roham","familyName":"Koohestani","name":"Koohestani, Roham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agnia","familyName":"Sergeyuk","name":"Sergeyuk, Agnia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Gros","name":"Gros, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Spiess","name":"Spiess, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Titov","name":"Titov, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prem","familyName":"Devanbu","name":"Devanbu, Prem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maliheh","familyName":"Izadi","name":"Izadi, Maliheh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Does In-IDE Calibration of Large Language Models work at Scale?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T10:15:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:57:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The introduction of large language models into integrated development environments (IDEs) is revolutionizing software engineering, yet it poses challenges to the usefulness and reliability of Artificial Intelligence-generated code. Post-hoc calibration of internal model confidences aims to align probabilities with an acceptability measure. Prior work suggests calibration can improve alignment, but at-scale evidence is limited. In this work, we investigate the feasibility of applying calibration of code models to an in-IDE context. We study two aspects of the problem: (1) the technical method for implementing confidence calibration and improving the reliability of code generation models, and (2) the human-centered design principles for effectively communicating reliability signal to developers. First, we develop a scalable and flexible calibration framework which can be used to obtain calibration weights for open-source models using any dataset, and evaluate whether calibrators improve the alignment between model confidence and developer acceptance behavior. Through a large-scale analysis of over 24 million real-world developer interactions across multiple programming languages, we find that a general, post-hoc calibration model based on Platt-scaling does not, on average, improve the reliability of model confidence signals. We also find that while dynamically personalizing calibration to individual users can be effective, its effectiveness is highly dependent on the volume of user interaction data. Second, we conduct a multi-phase design study with 3 expert designers and 153 professional developers, combining scenario-based design, semi-structured interviews, and survey validation, revealing a clear preference for presenting reliability signals via non-numerical, color-coded indicators within the in-editor code generation workflow."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22614","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:19:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:19:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:19:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahiro","familyName":"Ukai","name":"Ukai, Mahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhei","familyName":"Kurita","name":"Kurita, Shuhei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nakamasa","familyName":"Inoue","name":"Inoue, Nakamasa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"STATUS Bench: A Rigorous Benchmark for Evaluating Object State Understanding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T08:04:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:54:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Object state recognition aims to identify the specific condition of objects, such as their positional states (e.g., open or closed) and functional states (e.g., on or off). While recent Vision-Language Models (VLMs) are capable of performing a variety of multimodal tasks, it remains unclear how precisely they can identify object states. To alleviate this issue, we introduce the STAte and Transition UnderStanding Benchmark (STATUS Bench), the first benchmark for rigorously evaluating the ability of VLMs to understand subtle variations in object states in diverse situations. Specifically, STATUS Bench introduces a novel evaluation scheme that requires VLMs to perform three tasks simultaneously: object state identification (OSI), image retrieval (IR), and state change identification (SCI). These tasks are defined over our fully hand-crafted dataset involving image pairs, their corresponding object state descriptions and state change descriptions. Furthermore, we introduce a large-scale training dataset, namely STATUS Train, which consists of 13 million semi-automatically created descriptions. This dataset serves as the largest resource to facilitate further research in this area. In our experiments, we demonstrate that STATUS Bench enables rigorous consistency evaluation and reveal that current state-of-the-art VLMs still significantly struggle to capture subtle object state distinctions. Surprisingly, under the proposed rigorous evaluation scheme, most open-weight VLMs exhibited chance-level zero-shot performance. After fine-tuning on STATUS Train, Qwen2.5-VL achieved performance comparable to Gemini 2.0 Flash. These findings underscore the necessity of STATUS Bench and Train for advancing object state recognition in VLM research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:18:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:18:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:18:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bibhabasu","familyName":"Debnath","name":"Debnath, Bibhabasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahana","familyName":"Ray","name":"Ray, Sahana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Low-Light Image Enhancement Using Gamma Learning And Attention-Enabled Encoder-Decoder Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T06:09:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:52:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Images acquired in low-light environments present significant obstacles for computer vision systems and human perception, especially for applications requiring accurate object recognition and scene analysis. Such images typically manifest multiple quality issues: amplified noise, inadequate scene illumination, contrast reduction, color distortion, and loss of details. While recent deep learning methods have shown promise, developing simple and efficient frameworks that naturally integrate global illumination adjustment with local detail refinement continues to be an important objective. To this end, we introduce a dual-stage deep learning architecture that combines adaptive gamma correction with attention-enhanced refinement to address these fundamental limitations. The first stage uses an Adaptive Gamma Correction Module (AGCM) to learn suitable gamma values for each pixel based on both local and global cues, producing a brightened intermediate output. The second stage applies an encoder-decoder deep network with Convolutional Block Attention Modules (CBAM) to this brightened image, in order to restore finer details. We train the network using a composite loss that includes L1 reconstruction, SSIM, total variation, color constancy, and gamma regularization terms to balance pixel accuracy with visual quality. Experiments on LOL-v1, LOL-v2 real, and LOL-v2 synthetic datasets show our method reaches PSNR of upto 29.96 dB and upto 0.9458 SSIM, outperforming existing approaches. Additional tests on DICM, LIME, MEF, and NPE datasets using NIQE, BRISQUE, and UNIQUE metrics confirm better perceptual quality with fewer artifacts, achieving the best NIQE scores across all datasets. Our GAtED (Gamma learned and Attention-enabled Encoder-Decoder) method effectively handles both global illumination adjustment and local detail enhancement, offering a practical solution for low-light enhancement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, and 2 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:17:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:17:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:17:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22531","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22531","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22531","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noshitha Padma Pratyusha","familyName":"Juttu","name":"Juttu, Noshitha Padma Pratyusha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahithi","familyName":"Singireddy","name":"Singireddy, Sahithi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sravani","familyName":"Gona","name":"Gona, Sravani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujal","familyName":"Timilsina","name":"Timilsina, Sujal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Text to Trust: Evaluating Fine-Tuning and LoRA Trade-offs in Language Models for Unfair Terms of Service Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T04:46:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:51:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed text understanding, yet their adaptation to specialized legal domains remains constrained by the cost of full fine-tuning. This study provides a systematic evaluation of fine tuning, parameter efficient adaptation (LoRA, QLoRA), and zero-shot prompting strategies for unfair clause detection in Terms of Service (ToS) documents, a key application in legal NLP. We finetune BERT and DistilBERT, apply 4-bit Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) to models such as TinyLlama, LLaMA 3B/7B, and SaulLM, and evaluate GPT-4o and O-versions in zero-shot settings. Experiments on the CLAUDETTE-ToS benchmark and the Multilingual Scraper Corpus show that full fine-tuning achieves the strongest precision recall balance, while LoRA-based models provide competitive recall with up to 3x lower memory cost. These findings highlight practical design trade-offs for efficient and domain-adapted LLMs, contributing open baselines for fine-tuning research in legal text processing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, including figures and tables. All experiments are reproducible. Code and fine-tuned models are publicly available on: GitHub: (https://github.com/Stimils02/UnfairTOSAgreementsDetection) and Hugging Face: (https://huggingface.co/Noshitha98)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22531","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:17:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:17:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:17:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22489","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22489","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22489","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanhe","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuanhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xican","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xican","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haishan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Haishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Frustratingly Easy Task-aware Pruning for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T02:09:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:48:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pruning provides a practical solution to reduce the resources required to run large language models (LLMs) to benefit from their effective capabilities as well as control their cost for training and inference. Research on LLM pruning often ranks the importance of LLM parameters using their magnitudes and calibration-data activations and removes (or masks) the less important ones, accordingly reducing LLMs' size. However, these approaches primarily focus on preserving the LLM's ability to generate fluent sentences, while neglecting performance on specific domains and tasks. In this paper, we propose a simple yet effective pruning approach for LLMs that preserves task-specific capabilities while shrinking their parameter space. We first analyze how conventional pruning minimizes loss perturbation under general-domain calibration and extend this formulation by incorporating task-specific feature distributions into the importance computation of existing pruning algorithms. Thus, our framework computes separate importance scores using both general and task-specific calibration data, partitions parameters into shared and exclusive groups based on activation-norm differences, and then fuses their scores to guide the pruning process. This design enables our method to integrate seamlessly with various foundation pruning techniques and preserve the LLM's specialized abilities under compression. Experiments on widely used benchmarks demonstrate that our approach is effective and consistently outperforms the baselines with identical pruning ratios and different settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22489","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:16:34Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:16:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:16:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22488","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22488","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22488","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaowei","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunyan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Chunyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TLSQKT: A Question-Aware Dual-Channel Transformer for Literacy Tracing from Learning Sequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T02:07:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:47:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge tracing (KT) supports personalized learning by modeling how students' knowledge states evolve over time. However, most KT models emphasize mastery of discrete knowledge components, limiting their ability to characterize broader literacy development. We reframe the task as Literacy Tracing (LT), which models the growth of higher-order cognitive abilities and literacy from learners' interaction sequences, and we instantiate this paradigm with a Transformer-based model, TLSQKT (Transformer for Learning Sequences with Question-Aware Knowledge Tracing). TLSQKT employs a dual-channel design that jointly encodes student responses and item semantics, while question-aware interaction and self-attention capture long-range dependencies in learners' evolving states. Experiments on three real-world datasets - one public benchmark, one private knowledge-component dataset, and one private literacy dataset - show that TLSQKT consistently outperforms strong KT baselines on literacy-oriented metrics and reveals interpretable developmental trajectories of learners' literacy. Transfer experiments further indicate that knowledge-tracing signals can be leveraged for literacy tracing, offering a practical route when dedicated literacy labels are limited. These findings position literacy tracing as a scalable component of intelligent educational systems and lay the groundwork for literacy evaluation in future large-scale educational models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22488","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:16:33Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:16:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:16:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaitong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Kaitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijia","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yufeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keze","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Keze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Backward-Friendly Optimization: Training Large Language Models with Approximate Gradients under Memory Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T00:50:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:46:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Full fine-tuning of Large Language Models (LLMs) is notoriously memory-intensive, primarily because conventional optimizers such as SGD or Adam assume access to exact gradients derived from cached activations. Existing solutions either alter the model architecture (e.g., reversible networks) or trade memory for computation (e.g., activation checkpointing), but the optimizer itself remains untouched. In this work, we introduce GradLite, a backward-friendly optimizer that relaxes the requirement of exact gradients, enabling efficient training even when intermediate activations are aggressively discarded or approximated. GradLite leverages two key techniques: (i) low-rank Jacobian approximation, which reduces the dimensionality of backpropagated error signals, and (ii) error-feedback correction, which accumulates and compensates approximation errors across iterations to preserve convergence guarantees. We provide a theoretical analysis showing that GradLite maintains unbiased gradient estimates with bounded variance, ensuring convergence rates comparable to Adam. Empirically, GradLite reduces optimizer-state and activation memory consumption by up to 50\\% without architectural changes, and achieves on-par or superior downstream performance on reasoning (MMLU, GSM8K), multilingual, and dialogue benchmarks compared to checkpointing and optimizer-centric baselines (LoMo, GaLore)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:16:04Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:16:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:16:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22455","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22455","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22455","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon James","familyName":"Carone","name":"Carone, Brandon James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iran R.","familyName":"Roman","name":"Roman, Iran R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pablo","familyName":"Ripollés","name":"Ripollés, Pablo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Multimodal Large Language Models on Core Music Perception Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T23:10:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:46:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (LLMs) claim \"musical understanding\" via evaluations that conflate listening with score reading. We benchmark three SOTA LLMs (Gemini 2.5 Pro, Gemini 2.5 Flash, and Qwen2.5-Omni) across three core music skills: Syncopation Scoring, Transposition Detection, and Chord Quality Identification. Moreover, we separate three sources of variability: (i) perceptual limitations (audio vs. MIDI inputs), (ii) exposure to examples (zero- vs. few-shot manipulations), and (iii) reasoning strategies (Standalone, CoT, LogicLM). For the latter we adapt LogicLM, a framework combining LLMs with symbolic solvers to perform structured reasoning, to music. Results reveal a clear perceptual gap: models perform near ceiling on MIDI but show accuracy drops on audio. Reasoning and few-shot prompting offer minimal gains. This is expected for MIDI, where performance reaches saturation, but more surprising for audio, where LogicLM, despite near-perfect MIDI accuracy, remains notably brittle. Among models, Gemini Pro achieves the highest performance across most conditions. Overall, current systems reason well over symbols (MIDI) but do not yet \"listen\" reliably from audio. Our method and dataset make the perception-reasoning boundary explicit and offer actionable guidance for building robust, audio-first music systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on AI for Music (AI4Music), 16 pages, 1 figure, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22455","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:15:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:15:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:15:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22407","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22407","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22407","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sizhu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Sizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuran","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Qiuran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikos","familyName":"Vlassis","name":"Vlassis, Nikos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TERRA: A Transformer-Enabled Recursive R-learner for Longitudinal Heterogeneous Treatment Effect Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62M10, 62D20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T19:06:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:42:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurately estimating heterogeneous treatment effects (HTE) in longitudinal settings is essential for personalized decision-making across healthcare, public policy, education, and digital marketing. However, time-varying interventions introduce many unique challenges, such as carryover effects, time-varying heterogeneity, and post-treatment bias, which are not addressed by standard HTE methods. To address these challenges, we introduce TERRA (Transformer-Enabled Recursive R-learner), which facilitates longitudinal HTE estimation with flexible temporal modeling and learning. TERRA has two components. First, we use a Transformer architecture to encode full treatment-feature histories, enabling the representation of long-range temporal dependencies and carryover effects, hence capturing individual- and time-specific treatment effect variation more comprehensively. Second, we develop a recursive residual-learning formulation that generalizes the classical structural nested mean models (SNMMs) beyond parametric specifications, addressing post-treatment bias while reducing reliance on functional assumptions. In simulations and data applications, TERRA consistently outperforms strong baselines in HTE estimation in both accuracy and stability, highlighting the value of combining principled causal structure with high-capacity sequence models for longitudinal HTE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22407","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:14:39Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:14:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:14:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongliang","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Dongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PortGPT: Towards Automated Backporting Using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T18:46:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:42:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Patch backporting, the process of migrating mainline security patches to older branches, is an essential task in maintaining popular open-source projects (e.g., Linux kernel). However, manual backporting can be labor-intensive, while existing automated methods, which heavily rely on predefined syntax or semantic rules, often lack agility for complex patches.\n In this paper, we introduce PORTGPT, an LLM-agent for end-to-end automation of patch backporting in real-world scenarios. PORTGPT enhances an LLM with tools to access code on-demand, summarize Git history, and revise patches autonomously based on feedback (e.g., from compilers), hence, simulating human-like reasoning and verification. PORTGPT achieved an 89.15% success rate on existing datasets (1815 cases), and 62.33% on our own dataset of 146 complex cases, both outperforms state-of-the-art of backporting tools. We contributed 9 backported patches from PORTGPT to the Linux kernel community and all patches are now merged."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE S\u0026amp;P 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:14:23Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:14:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:14:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22369","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22369","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22369","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Egor","familyName":"Kolodin","name":"Kolodin, Egor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daria","familyName":"Khomich","name":"Khomich, Daria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Savushkin","name":"Savushkin, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasia","familyName":"Ianina","name":"Ianina, Anastasia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fyodor","familyName":"Minkin","name":"Minkin, Fyodor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GigaEmbeddings: Efficient Russian Language Embedding Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T17:26:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce GigaEmbeddings, a novel framework for training high-performance Russian-focused text embeddings through hierarchical instruction tuning of the decoder-only LLM designed specifically for Russian language (GigaChat-3B). Our three-stage pipeline, comprising large-scale contrastive pre-training in web-scale corpora, fine-tuning with hard negatives, and multitask generalization across retrieval, classification, and clustering tasks, addresses key limitations of existing methods by unifying diverse objectives and leveraging synthetic data generation. Architectural innovations include bidirectional attention for contextual modeling, latent attention pooling for robust sequence aggregation, and strategic pruning of 25% of transformer layers to enhance efficiency without compromising performance. Evaluated on the ruMTEB benchmark spanning 23 multilingual tasks, GigaEmbeddings achieves state-of-the-art results (69.1 avg. score), outperforming strong baselines with a larger number of parameters."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22369","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:45Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiazheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Damianou","name":"Damianou, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J","familyName":"Rosser","name":"Rosser, J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"José Luis Redondo","familyName":"García","name":"García, José Luis Redondo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantina","familyName":"Palla","name":"Palla, Konstantina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mapping Faithful Reasoning in Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T16:48:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) traces promise transparency for reasoning language models, but prior work shows they are not always faithful reflections of internal computation. This raises challenges for oversight: practitioners may misinterpret decorative reasoning as genuine. We introduce Concept Walk, a general framework for tracing how a model's internal stance evolves with respect to a concept direction during reasoning. Unlike surface text, Concept Walk operates in activation space, projecting each reasoning step onto the concept direction learned from contrastive data. This allows us to observe whether reasoning traces shape outcomes or are discarded. As a case study, we apply Concept Walk to the domain of Safety using Qwen 3-4B. We find that in 'easy' cases, perturbed CoTs are quickly ignored, indicating decorative reasoning, whereas in 'hard' cases, perturbations induce sustained shifts in internal activations, consistent with faithful reasoning. The contribution is methodological: Concept Walk provides a lens to re-examine faithfulness through concept-specific internal dynamics, helping identify when reasoning traces can be trusted and when they risk misleading practitioners."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, Accepted to the Mechanistic Interpretability Workshop at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:35Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22359","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22359","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22359","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Changhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew J.","familyName":"Clarkson","name":"Clarkson, Matthew J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mobarak I.","familyName":"Hoque","name":"Hoque, Mobarak I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EndoSfM3D: Learning to 3D Reconstruct Any Endoscopic Surgery Scene using Self-supervised Foundation Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T16:39:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D reconstruction of endoscopic surgery scenes plays a vital role in enhancing scene perception, enabling AR visualization, and supporting context-aware decision-making in image-guided surgery. A critical yet challenging step in this process is the accurate estimation of the endoscope's intrinsic parameters. In real surgical settings, intrinsic calibration is hindered by sterility constraints and the use of specialized endoscopes with continuous zoom and telescope rotation. Most existing methods for endoscopic 3D reconstruction do not estimate intrinsic parameters, limiting their effectiveness for accurate and reliable reconstruction. In this paper, we integrate intrinsic parameter estimation into a self-supervised monocular depth estimation framework by adapting the Depth Anything V2 (DA2) model for joint depth, pose, and intrinsics prediction. We introduce an attention-based pose network and a Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation (DoRA) strategy for efficient fine-tuning of DA2. Our method is validated on the SCARED and C3VD public datasets, demonstrating superior performance compared to recent state-of-the-art approaches in self-supervised monocular depth estimation and 3D reconstruction. Code and model weights can be found in project repository: https://github.com/MOYF-beta/EndoSfM3D."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22359","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:31Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22356","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22356","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22356","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fiaz","familyName":"Ahmad","name":"Ahmad, Fiaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nisar","familyName":"Hussain","name":"Hussain, Nisar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amna","familyName":"Qasim","name":"Qasim, Amna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Momina","familyName":"Hafeez","name":"Hafeez, Momina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Usman Grigori","familyName":"Sidorov","name":"Sidorov, Muhammad Usman Grigori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Gelbukh","name":"Gelbukh, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Irony Detection in Urdu Text: A Comparative Study Using Machine Learning Models and Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T16:36:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ironic identification is a challenging task in Natural Language Processing, particularly when dealing with languages that differ in syntax and cultural context. In this work, we aim to detect irony in Urdu by translating an English Ironic Corpus into the Urdu language. We evaluate ten state-of-the-art machine learning algorithms using GloVe and Word2Vec embeddings, and compare their performance with classical methods. Additionally, we fine-tune advanced transformer-based models, including BERT, RoBERTa, LLaMA 2 (7B), LLaMA 3 (8B), and Mistral, to assess the effectiveness of large-scale models in irony detection. Among machine learning models, Gradient Boosting achieved the best performance with an F1-score of 89.18%. Among transformer-based models, LLaMA 3 (8B) achieved the highest performance with an F1-score of 94.61%. These results demonstrate that combining transliteration techniques with modern NLP models enables robust irony detection in Urdu, a historically low-resource language."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 3 figuers"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22356","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:26Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22332","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22332","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22332","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Suzuki","name":"Suzuki, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuro","familyName":"Inaba","name":"Inaba, Tatsuro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuki","familyName":"Kuribayashi","name":"Kuribayashi, Tatsuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transformer Key-Value Memories Are Nearly as Interpretable as Sparse Autoencoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T15:34:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent interpretability work on large language models (LLMs) has been increasingly dominated by a feature-discovery approach with the help of proxy modules. Then, the quality of features learned by, e.g., sparse auto-encoders (SAEs), is evaluated. This paradigm naturally raises a critical question: do such learned features have better properties than those already represented within the original model parameters, and unfortunately, only a few studies have made such comparisons systematically so far. In this work, we revisit the interpretability of feature vectors stored in feed-forward (FF) layers, given the perspective of FF as key-value memories, with modern interpretability benchmarks. Our extensive evaluation revealed that SAE and FFs exhibits a similar range of interpretability, although SAEs displayed an observable but minimal improvement in some aspects. Furthermore, in certain aspects, surprisingly, even vanilla FFs yielded better interpretability than the SAEs, and features discovered in SAEs and FFs diverged. These bring questions about the advantage of SAEs from both perspectives of feature quality and faithfulness, compared to directly interpreting FF feature vectors, and FF key-value parameters serve as a strong baseline in modern interpretability research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22332","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:12:51Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:12:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:12:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan-Phong","familyName":"Ngo","name":"Ngo, Tuan-Phong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bao-Ngoc","familyName":"Duong","name":"Duong, Bao-Ngoc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan-Anh","familyName":"Hoang","name":"Hoang, Tuan-Anh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua","familyName":"Dwight","name":"Dwight, Joshua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ushik Shrestha","familyName":"Khwakhali","name":"Khwakhali, Ushik Shrestha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harnessing the Power of Large Language Models for Software Testing Education: A Focus on ISTQB Syllabus"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"K.3.2, D.2.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T14:45:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:38:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Software testing is a critical component in the software engineering field and is important for software engineering education. Thus, it is vital for academia to continuously improve and update educational methods to reflect the current state of the field. The International Software Testing Qualifications Board (ISTQB) certification framework is globally recognized and widely adopted in industry and academia. However, ISTQB-based learning has been rarely applied with recent generative artificial intelligence advances. Despite the growing capabilities of large language models (LLMs), ISTQB-based learning and instruction with LLMs have not been thoroughly explored. This paper explores and evaluates how LLMs can complement the ISTQB framework for higher education. The findings present four key contributions: (i) the creation of a comprehensive ISTQB-aligned dataset spanning over a decade, consisting of 28 sample exams and 1,145 questions; (ii) the development of a domain-optimized prompt that enhances LLM precision and explanation quality on ISTQB tasks; (iii) a systematic evaluation of state-of-the-art LLMs on this dataset; and (iv) actionable insights and recommendations for integrating LLMs into software testing education. These findings highlight the promise of LLMs in supporting ISTQB certification preparation and offer a foundation for their broader use in software engineering at higher education."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:12:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:12:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:12:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antal van den","familyName":"Bosch","name":"Bosch, Antal van den","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ainhoa Risco","familyName":"Patón","name":"Patón, Ainhoa Risco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teun","familyName":"Buijse","name":"Buijse, Teun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Berck","name":"Berck, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"van Gompel","name":"van Gompel, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Memory-based Language Models: An Efficient, Explainable, and Eco-friendly Approach to Large Language Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T14:34:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present memory-based language modeling as an efficient, eco-friendly alternative to deep neural network-based language modeling. It offers log-linearly scalable next-token prediction performance and strong memorization capabilities. Implementing fast approximations of k-nearest neighbor classification, memory-based language modeling leaves a relatively small ecological footprint both in training and in inference mode, as it relies fully on CPUs and attains low token latencies. Its internal workings are simple and fully transparent. We compare our implementation of memory-based language modeling, OLIFANT, with GPT-2 and GPT-Neo on next-token prediction accuracy, estimated emissions and speeds, and offer some deeper analyses of the model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:12:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:12:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:12:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22312","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22312","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22312","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LacMaterial: Large Language Models as Analogical Chemists for Materials Discovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T14:25:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:37:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Analogical reasoning, the transfer of relational structures across contexts (e.g., planet is to sun as electron is to nucleus), is fundamental to scientific discovery. Yet human insight is often constrained by domain expertise and surface-level biases, limiting access to deeper, structure-driven analogies both within and across disciplines. Large language models (LLMs), trained on vast cross-domain data, present a promising yet underexplored tool for analogical reasoning in science. Here, we demonstrate that LLMs can generate novel battery materials by (1) retrieving cross-domain analogs and analogy-guided exemplars to steer exploration beyond conventional dopant substitutions, and (2) constructing in-domain analogical templates from few labeled examples to guide targeted exploitation. These explicit analogical reasoning strategies yield candidates outside established compositional spaces and outperform standard prompting baselines. Our findings position LLMs as interpretable, expert-like hypothesis generators that leverage analogy-driven generalization for scientific innovation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22312","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:12:23Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:12:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:12:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22301","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22301","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22301","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujie","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongzhen","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Gongzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangkun","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Guangkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoran","familyName":"Kan","name":"Kan, Zhuoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deyun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Deyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenda","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Shenda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AnyECG-Lab: An Exploration Study of Fine-tuning an ECG Foundation Model to Estimate Laboratory Values from Single-Lead ECG Signals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T14:04:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:37:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Timely access to laboratory values is critical for clinical decision-making, yet current approaches rely on invasive venous sampling and are intrinsically delayed. Electrocardiography (ECG), as a non-invasive and widely available signal, offers a promising modality for rapid laboratory estimation. Recent progress in deep learning has enabled the extraction of latent hematological signatures from ECGs. However, existing models are constrained by low signal-to-noise ratios, substantial inter-individual variability, limited data diversity, and suboptimal generalization, especially when adapted to low-lead wearable devices. In this work, we conduct an exploratory study leveraging transfer learning to fine-tune ECGFounder, a large-scale pre-trained ECG foundation model, on the Multimodal Clinical Monitoring in the Emergency Department (MC-MED) dataset from Stanford. We generated a corpus of more than 20 million standardized ten-second ECG segments to enhance sensitivity to subtle biochemical correlates. On internal validation, the model demonstrated strong predictive performance (area under the curve above 0.65) for thirty-three laboratory indicators, moderate performance (between 0.55 and 0.65) for fifty-nine indicators, and limited performance (below 0.55) for sixteen indicators. This study provides an efficient artificial-intelligence driven solution and establishes the feasibility scope for real-time, non-invasive estimation of laboratory values."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22301","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:12:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:12:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:12:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Berkay","familyName":"Döner","name":"Döner, Berkay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thorir Mar","familyName":"Ingolfsson","name":"Ingolfsson, Thorir Mar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Benini","name":"Benini, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LUNA: Efficient and Topology-Agnostic Foundation Model for EEG Signal Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T11:31:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:34:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electroencephalography (EEG) offers a non-invasive lens into human brain activity, but building large-scale models is hampered by topological heterogeneity: each public EEG data defines its own electrode layout, limiting generalization. We introduce LUNA (Latent Unified Network Architecture), a self-supervised foundation model that reconciles disparate electrode geometries while scaling linearly -- not quadratically -- with channel count. LUNA compresses multi-channel EEG into a fixed-size, topology-agnostic latent space via learned queries and cross-attention. Downstream transformer blocks then operate exclusively on this latent representation using patch-wise temporal self-attention, decoupling computation from electrode count. Pre-trained on TUEG and Siena (over 21,000 hours of raw EEG across diverse montages) using a masked-patch reconstruction objective, LUNA transfers effectively to four downstream tasks: abnormality detection, artifact rejection, slowing classification, and emotion recognition. It demonstrates highly competitive performance across several benchmarks, achieving state-of-the-art results on TUAR and TUSL, e.g., 0.921 AUROC on TUAR, while reducing FLOPs by 300x and trimming GPU memory use by up to 10x. Critically, these gains are consistent across all evaluated electrode configurations. Code is available at https://github.com/pulp-bio/BioFoundation"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS camera-ready version, 27 pages, 10 figures, 13 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:11:04Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:11:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:11:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":128,"totalPages":2,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-27T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-28T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+attention+OR+MoE+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-27T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-28T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+attention+OR+MoE+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29%29"}}